{"surah": 19, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "كٓهيعٓصٓ ", "translation": "Kāf, Hā, Yā, ʿAyn, Ṣād.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كٓهيعٓصٓ", "translit": "ٓṣٓʿعyhٓk", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ذِكْرُ رَحْمَتِ رَبِّكَ عَبْدَهُۥ زَكَرِيَّآ ", "translation": "[This is] a mention of the mercy of your Lord to His servant Zechariah", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذِكْرُ", "translit": "ُrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَحْمَتِ", "translit": "ِtَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَبْدَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdْbَʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "زَكَرِيَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّyِrَkَz", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "إِذْ نَادَىٰ رَبَّهُۥ نِدَآءً خَفِيًّا ", "translation": "When he called to his Lord a private call [i.e., supplication].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَادَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdāَn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نِدَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَdِn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَفِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِfَhk", "root": "خفي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّ إِنِّى وَهَنَ ٱلْعَظْمُ مِنِّى وَٱشْتَعَلَ ٱلرَّأْسُ شَيْبًا وَلَمْ أَكُنۢ بِدُعَآئِكَ رَبِّ شَقِيًّا ", "translation": "He said, \"My Lord, indeed my bones have weakened, and my head has filled[1] with white, and never have I been in my supplication to You, my Lord, unhappy [i.e., disappointed].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهَنَ", "translit": "َnَhَw", "root": "وهن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْعَظْمُ", "translit": "ُmْẓَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱشْتَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَtْhsٱَw", "root": "شعل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلرَّأْسُ", "translit": "ُsْaʾَّrlٱ", "root": "راس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَيْبًا", "translit": "āًbْyَhs", "root": "شيب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَكُنۢ", "translit": "ۢnُkَaʾ", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِدُعَآئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾٓāَʿعُdِb", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "شَقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqَhs", "root": "شقو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَإِنِّى خِفْتُ ٱلْمَوَٰلِىَ مِن وَرَآءِى وَكَانَتِ ٱمْرَأَتِى عَاقِرًا فَهَبْ لِى مِن لَّدُنكَ وَلِيًّا ", "translation": "And indeed, I fear the successors[1] after me, and my wife has been barren, so give me from Yourself an heir", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خِفْتُ", "translit": "ُtْfِhk", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوَٰلِىَ", "translit": "َàِlَٰwَmْlٱ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَرَآءِى", "translit": "àِءٓāَrَw", "root": "وري"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكَانَتِ", "translit": "ِtَnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱمْرَأَتِى", "translit": "àِtَaʾَrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَاقِرًا", "translit": "āًrِqāَʿع", "root": "عقر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَهَبْ", "translit": "ْbَhَf", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِى", "translit": "àِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّدُنكَ", "translit": "َknُdَّl", "root": "لدن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlَw", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "يَرِثُنِى وَيَرِثُ مِنْ ءَالِ يَعْقُوبَۖ وَٱجْعَلْهُ رَبِّ رَضِيًّا ", "translation": "Who will inherit me[1] and inherit from the family of Jacob. And make him, my Lord, pleasing [to You].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَرِثُنِى", "translit": "àِnُhtِrَy", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَيَرِثُ", "translit": "ُhtِrَyَw", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَالِ", "translit": "ِlāَء", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَعْقُوبَ", "translit": "َbwُqْʿعَy", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱجْعَلْهُ", "translit": "ُhْlَʿعْjgٱَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَضِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِḍَr", "root": "رضو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "يَٰزَكَرِيَّآ إِنَّا نُبَشِّرُكَ بِغُلَٰمٍ ٱسْمُهُۥ يَحْيَىٰ لَمْ نَجْعَل لَّهُۥ مِن قَبْلُ سَمِيًّا ", "translation": "[He was told],[1] \"O Zechariah, indeed We give you good tidings of a boy whose name will be John. We have not assigned to any before [this] name.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰزَكَرِيَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّyِrَkَzَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُبَشِّرُكَ", "translit": "َkُrِّhsَbُn", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِغُلَٰمٍ", "translit": "ٍmَٰlُغِb", "root": "غلم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱسْمُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُmْsٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَحْيَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَyْḥَy", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَجْعَل", "translit": "lَʿعْjgَn", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سَمِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِmَs", "root": "سمو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّ أَنَّىٰ يَكُونُ لِى غُلَٰمٌ وَكَانَتِ ٱمْرَأَتِى عَاقِرًا وَقَدْ بَلَغْتُ مِنَ ٱلْكِبَرِ عِتِيًّا ", "translation": "He said, \"My Lord, how will I have a boy when my wife has been barren and I have reached extreme old age?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَكُونُ", "translit": "ُnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِى", "translit": "àِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "غُلَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰlُغ", "root": "غلم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكَانَتِ", "translit": "ِtَnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱمْرَأَتِى", "translit": "àِtَaʾَrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَاقِرًا", "translit": "āًrِqāَʿع", "root": "عقر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَلَغْتُ", "translit": "ُtْغَlَb", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْكِبَرِ", "translit": "ِrَbِkْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِتِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِtِʿع", "root": "عتو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "قَالَ كَذَٰلِكَ قَالَ رَبُّكَ هُوَ عَلَىَّ هَيِّنٌ وَقَدْ خَلَقْتُكَ مِن قَبْلُ وَلَمْ تَكُ شَيْـًٔا ", "translation": "[An angel] said, \"Thus [it will be]; your Lord says, 'It is easy for Me, for I created you before, while you were nothing.'\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هَيِّنٌ", "translit": "ٌnِّyَh", "root": "هون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَلَقْتُكَ", "translit": "َkُtْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَكُ", "translit": "ُkَt", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّ ٱجْعَل لِّىٓ ءَايَةًۚ قَالَ ءَايَتُكَ أَلَّا تُكَلِّمَ ٱلنَّاسَ ثَلَٰثَ لَيَالٍ سَوِيًّا ", "translation": "[Zechariah] said, \"My Lord, make for me a sign.\" He said, \"Your sign is that you will not speak to the people for three nights, [being] sound.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱجْعَل", "translit": "lَʿعْjgٱ", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَايَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَايَتُكَ", "translit": "َkُtَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تُكَلِّمَ", "translit": "َmِّlَkُt", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ثَلَٰثَ", "translit": "َhtَٰlَht", "root": "ثلث"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَيَالٍ", "translit": "ٍlāَyَl", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَوِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِwَs", "root": "سوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "فَخَرَجَ عَلَىٰ قَوْمِهِۦ مِنَ ٱلْمِحْرَابِ فَأَوْحَىٰٓ إِلَيْهِمْ أَن سَبِّحُوا۟ بُكْرَةً وَعَشِيًّا ", "translation": "So he came out to his people from the prayer chamber and signaled to them to exalt [Allāh] in the morning and afternoon.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَخَرَجَ", "translit": "َjgَrَhkَf", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمِحْرَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَrْḥِmْlٱ", "root": "حرب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَوْحَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḥْwَaʾَf", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَبِّحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِّbَs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بُكْرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrْkُb", "root": "بكر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَعَشِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِhsَʿعَw", "root": "عشو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "يَٰيَحْيَىٰ خُذِ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ بِقُوَّةٍۖ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ ٱلْحُكْمَ صَبِيًّا ", "translation": "[Allāh said], \"O John, take the Scripture [i.e., adhere to it] with determination.\" And We gave him judgement [while yet] a boy", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰيَحْيَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَyْḥَyَٰy", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خُذِ", "translit": "ِhdُhk", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِقُوَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّwُqِb", "root": "قوي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحُكْمَ", "translit": "َmْkُḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صَبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَṣ", "root": "صبو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَحَنَانًا مِّن لَّدُنَّا وَزَكَوٰةًۖ وَكَانَ تَقِيًّا ", "translation": "And affection from Us and purity, and he was fearing of Allāh", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَحَنَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَnَḥَw", "root": "حنن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَّدُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُdَّl", "root": "لدن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَزَكَوٰةً", "translit": "ًẗٰwَkَzَw", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "وَبَرًّۢا بِوَٰلِدَيْهِ وَلَمْ يَكُن جَبَّارًا عَصِيًّا ", "translation": "And dutiful to his parents, and he was not a disobedient tyrant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَبَرًّۢا", "translit": "āًّۢrَbَw", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِوَٰلِدَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَdِlَٰwِb", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَكُن", "translit": "nُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَبَّارًا", "translit": "āًrāَّbَjg", "root": "جبر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَصِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِṣَʿع", "root": "عصي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "وَسَلَٰمٌ عَلَيْهِ يَوْمَ وُلِدَ وَيَوْمَ يَمُوتُ وَيَوْمَ يُبْعَثُ حَيًّا ", "translation": "And peace be upon him the day he was born and the day he dies and the day he is raised alive.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَسَلَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰlَsَw", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وُلِدَ", "translit": "َdِlُw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَمُوتُ", "translit": "ُtwُmَy", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُبْعَثُ", "translit": "ُhtَʿعْbُy", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حَيًّا", "translit": "āًّyَḥ", "root": "حيي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَٱذْكُرْ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ مَرْيَمَ إِذِ ٱنتَبَذَتْ مِنْ أَهْلِهَا مَكَانًا شَرْقِيًّا ", "translation": "And mention, [O Muḥammad], in the Book [the story of] Mary, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱذْكُرْ", "translit": "ْrُkْhdٱَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِذِ", "translit": "ِhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱنتَبَذَتْ", "translit": "ْtَhdَbَtnٱ", "root": "نبذ"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَهْلِهَا", "translit": "āَhِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَكَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَkَm", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَرْقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqْrَhs", "root": "شرق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "فَٱتَّخَذَتْ مِن دُونِهِمْ حِجَابًا فَأَرْسَلْنَآ إِلَيْهَا رُوحَنَا فَتَمَثَّلَ لَهَا بَشَرًا سَوِيًّا ", "translation": "And she took, in seclusion from them, a screen. Then We sent to her Our Angel [i.e., Gabriel], and he represented himself to her as a well-proportioned man.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱتَّخَذَتْ", "translit": "ْtَhdَhkَّtٱَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "دُونِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حِجَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَjgِḥ", "root": "حجب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَأَرْسَلْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْlَsْrَaʾَf", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رُوحَنَا", "translit": "āَnَḥwُr", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَتَمَثَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّhtَmَtَf", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَشَرًا", "translit": "āًrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَوِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِwَs", "root": "سوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "قَالَتْ إِنِّىٓ أَعُوذُ بِٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ مِنكَ إِن كُنتَ تَقِيًّا ", "translation": "She said, \"Indeed, I seek refuge in the Most Merciful from you, [so leave me], if you should be fearing of Allāh.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَعُوذُ", "translit": "ُhdwُʿعَaʾ", "root": "عوذ"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱِb", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنكَ", "translit": "َknِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كُنتَ", "translit": "َtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "قَالَ إِنَّمَآ أَنَا۠ رَسُولُ رَبِّكِ لِأَهَبَ لَكِ غُلَٰمًا زَكِيًّا ", "translation": "He said, \"I am only the messenger of your Lord to give you [news of] a pure boy [i.e., son].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَسُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّكِ", "translit": "ِkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِأَهَبَ", "translit": "َbَhَaʾِl", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَكِ", "translit": "ِkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "غُلَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰlُغ", "root": "غلم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "زَكِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِkَz", "root": "زكو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "قَالَتْ أَنَّىٰ يَكُونُ لِى غُلَٰمٌ وَلَمْ يَمْسَسْنِى بَشَرٌ وَلَمْ أَكُ بَغِيًّا ", "translation": "She said, \"How can I have a boy while no man has touched me and I have not been unchaste?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَكُونُ", "translit": "ُnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِى", "translit": "àِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "غُلَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰlُغ", "root": "غلم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَمْسَسْنِى", "translit": "àِnْsَsْmَy", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَشَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَكُ", "translit": "ُkَaʾ", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَغِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِغَb", "root": "بغي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "قَالَ كَذَٰلِكِ قَالَ رَبُّكِ هُوَ عَلَىَّ هَيِّنٌۖ وَلِنَجْعَلَهُۥٓ ءَايَةً لِّلنَّاسِ وَرَحْمَةً مِّنَّاۚ وَكَانَ أَمْرًا مَّقْضِيًّا ", "translation": "He said, \"Thus [it will be]; your Lord says, 'It is easy for Me, and We will make him a sign to the people and a mercy from Us. And it is a matter [already] decreed.'\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكِ", "translit": "ِkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبُّكِ", "translit": "ِkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هَيِّنٌ", "translit": "ٌnِّyَh", "root": "هون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلِنَجْعَلَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlَʿعْjgَnِlَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ءَايَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِّلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِّl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَرَحْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْḥَrَw", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِّنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَمْرًا", "translit": "āًrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَّقْضِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِḍْqَّm", "root": "قضي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "فَحَمَلَتْهُ فَٱنتَبَذَتْ بِهِۦ مَكَانًا قَصِيًّا ", "translation": "So she conceived him, and she withdrew with him to a remote place.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَحَمَلَتْهُ", "translit": "ُhْtَlَmَḥَf", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَٱنتَبَذَتْ", "translit": "ْtَhdَbَtnٱَf", "root": "نبذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَكَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَkَm", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَصِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِṣَq", "root": "قصو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "فَأَجَآءَهَا ٱلْمَخَاضُ إِلَىٰ جِذْعِ ٱلنَّخْلَةِ قَالَتْ يَٰلَيْتَنِى مِتُّ قَبْلَ هَٰذَا وَكُنتُ نَسْيًا مَّنسِيًّا ", "translation": "And the pains of childbirth drove her to the trunk of a palm tree. She said, \"Oh, I wish I had died before this and was in oblivion, forgotten.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَجَآءَهَا", "translit": "āَhَءٓāَjgَaʾَf", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمَخَاضُ", "translit": "ُḍāَhkَmْlٱ", "root": "مخض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جِذْعِ", "translit": "ِʿعْhdِjg", "root": "جذع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلنَّخْلَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَlْhkَّnlٱ", "root": "نخل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَالَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَٰلَيْتَنِى", "translit": "àِnَtْyَlَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِتُّ", "translit": "ُّtِm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَبْلَ", "translit": "َlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَكُنتُ", "translit": "ُtnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَسْيًا", "translit": "āًyْsَn", "root": "نسي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَّنسِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِsnَّm", "root": "نسي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "فَنَادَىٰهَا مِن تَحْتِهَآ أَلَّا تَحْزَنِى قَدْ جَعَلَ رَبُّكِ تَحْتَكِ سَرِيًّا ", "translation": "But he[1] called her from below her, \"Do not grieve; your Lord has provided beneath you a stream.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَنَادَىٰهَا", "translit": "āَhٰàَdāَnَf", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَحْزَنِى", "translit": "àِnَzْḥَt", "root": "حزن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبُّكِ", "translit": "ِkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَحْتَكِ", "translit": "ِkَtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "سَرِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِrَs", "root": "سري"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "وَهُزِّىٓ إِلَيْكِ بِجِذْعِ ٱلنَّخْلَةِ تُسَٰقِطْ عَلَيْكِ رُطَبًا جَنِيًّا ", "translation": "And shake toward you the trunk of the palm tree; it will drop upon you ripe, fresh dates.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُزِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّzُhَw", "root": "هزز"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَيْكِ", "translit": "ِkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِجِذْعِ", "translit": "ِʿعْhdِjgِb", "root": "جذع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلنَّخْلَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَlْhkَّnlٱ", "root": "نخل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُسَٰقِطْ", "translit": "ْṭِqَٰsُt", "root": "سقط"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْكِ", "translit": "ِkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رُطَبًا", "translit": "āًbَṭُr", "root": "رطب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَنِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِnَjg", "root": "جني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "فَكُلِى وَٱشْرَبِى وَقَرِّى عَيْنًاۖ فَإِمَّا تَرَيِنَّ مِنَ ٱلْبَشَرِ أَحَدًا فَقُولِىٓ إِنِّى نَذَرْتُ لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ صَوْمًا فَلَنْ أُكَلِّمَ ٱلْيَوْمَ إِنسِيًّا ", "translation": "So eat and drink and be contented. And if you see from among humanity anyone, say, 'Indeed, I have vowed to the Most Merciful abstention, so I will not speak today to [any] man.'\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكُلِى", "translit": "àِlُkَf", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَٱشْرَبِى", "translit": "àِbَrْhsٱَw", "root": "شرب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَقَرِّى", "translit": "àِّrَqَw", "root": "قرر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَيْنًا", "translit": "āًnْyَʿع", "root": "عين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَإِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَرَيِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِyَrَt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْبَشَرِ", "translit": "ِrَhsَbْlٱ", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَحَدًا", "translit": "āًdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَقُولِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِlwُqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَذَرْتُ", "translit": "ُtْrَhdَn", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlِl", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "صَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَṣ", "root": "صوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَلَنْ", "translit": "ْnَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أُكَلِّمَ", "translit": "َmِّlَkُaʾ", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِنسِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِsnِiʾ", "root": "انس"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "فَأَتَتْ بِهِۦ قَوْمَهَا تَحْمِلُهُۥۖ قَالُوا۟ يَٰمَرْيَمُ لَقَدْ جِئْتِ شَيْـًٔا فَرِيًّا ", "translation": "Then she brought him to her people, carrying him. They said, \"O Mary, you have certainly done a thing unprecedented.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَتَتْ", "translit": "ْtَtَaʾَf", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَوْمَهَا", "translit": "āَhَmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَحْمِلُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُlِmْḥَt", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَٰمَرْيَمُ", "translit": "ُmَyْrَmَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جِئْتِ", "translit": "ِtْyʾِjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَرِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِrَf", "root": "فري"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "يَٰٓأُخْتَ هَٰرُونَ مَا كَانَ أَبُوكِ ٱمْرَأَ سَوْءٍ وَمَا كَانَتْ أُمُّكِ بَغِيًّا ", "translation": "O sister [i.e., descendant] of Aaron, your father was not a man of evil, nor was your mother unchaste.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأُخْتَ", "translit": "َtْhkُaʾَٰٓy", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَبُوكِ", "translit": "ِkwُbَaʾ", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱمْرَأَ", "translit": "َaʾَrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "سَوْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْwَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَانَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أُمُّكِ", "translit": "ِkُّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَغِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِغَb", "root": "بغي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "فَأَشَارَتْ إِلَيْهِۖ قَالُوا۟ كَيْفَ نُكَلِّمُ مَن كَانَ فِى ٱلْمَهْدِ صَبِيًّا ", "translation": "So she pointed to him. They said, \"How can we speak to one who is in the cradle a child?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَشَارَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrāَhsَaʾَf", "root": "شور"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُكَلِّمُ", "translit": "ُmِّlَkُn", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمَهْدِ", "translit": "ِdْhَmْlٱ", "root": "مهد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "صَبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَṣ", "root": "صبو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "قَالَ إِنِّى عَبْدُ ٱللَّهِ ءَاتَىٰنِىَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَجَعَلَنِى نَبِيًّا ", "translation": "[Jesus] said, \"Indeed, I am the servant of Allāh. He has given me the Scripture and made me a prophet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَبْدُ", "translit": "ُdْbَʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَاتَىٰنِىَ", "translit": "َàِnٰàَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَجَعَلَنِى", "translit": "àِnَlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَجَعَلَنِى مُبَارَكًا أَيْنَ مَا كُنتُ وَأَوْصَٰنِى بِٱلصَّلَوٰةِ وَٱلزَّكَوٰةِ مَا دُمْتُ حَيًّا ", "translation": "And He has made me blessed wherever I am and has enjoined upon me prayer and zakāh as long as I remain alive", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلَنِى", "translit": "àِnَlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُبَارَكًا", "translit": "āًkَrāَbُm", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُنتُ", "translit": "ُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَوْصَٰنِى", "translit": "àِnَٰṣْwَaʾَw", "root": "وصي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlٱِb", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلزَّكَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَkَّzlٱَw", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "دُمْتُ", "translit": "ُtْmُd", "root": "دوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "حَيًّا", "translit": "āًّyَḥ", "root": "حيي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "وَبَرًّۢا بِوَٰلِدَتِى وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْنِى جَبَّارًا شَقِيًّا ", "translation": "And [made me] dutiful to my mother, and He has not made me a wretched tyrant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَبَرًّۢا", "translit": "āًّۢrَbَw", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِوَٰلِدَتِى", "translit": "àِtَdِlَٰwِb", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَجْعَلْنِى", "translit": "àِnْlَʿعْjgَy", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَبَّارًا", "translit": "āًrāَّbَjg", "root": "جبر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شَقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqَhs", "root": "شقو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "وَٱلسَّلَٰمُ عَلَىَّ يَوْمَ وُلِدتُّ وَيَوْمَ أَمُوتُ وَيَوْمَ أُبْعَثُ حَيًّا ", "translation": "And peace is on me the day I was born and the day I will die and the day I am raised alive.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلسَّلَٰمُ", "translit": "ُmَٰlَّslٱَw", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وُلِدتُّ", "translit": "ُّtdِlُw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَمُوتُ", "translit": "ُtwُmَaʾ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُبْعَثُ", "translit": "ُhtَʿعْbُaʾ", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حَيًّا", "translit": "āًّyَḥ", "root": "حيي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ عِيسَى ٱبْنُ مَرْيَمَۚ قَوْلَ ٱلْحَقِّ ٱلَّذِى فِيهِ يَمْتَرُونَ ", "translation": "That is Jesus, the son of Mary - the word of truth about which they are in dispute.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عِيسَى", "translit": "àَsyِʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱبْنُ", "translit": "ُnْbٱ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَوْلَ", "translit": "َlْwَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَمْتَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَtْmَy", "root": "مري"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "مَا كَانَ لِلَّهِ أَن يَتَّخِذَ مِن وَلَدٍۖ سُبْحَٰنَهُۥٓۚ إِذَا قَضَىٰٓ أَمْرًا فَإِنَّمَا يَقُولُ لَهُۥ كُن فَيَكُونُ ", "translation": "It is not [befitting] for Allāh to take a son; exalted is He![1] When He decrees an affair, He only says to it, \"Be,\" and it is.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَتَّخِذَ", "translit": "َhdِhkَّtَy", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَضَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḍَq", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَمْرًا", "translit": "āًrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "كُن", "translit": "nُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَيَكُونُ", "translit": "ُnwُkَyَf", "root": "كون"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ رَبِّى وَرَبُّكُمْ فَٱعْبُدُوهُۚ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌ مُّسْتَقِيمٌ ", "translation": "[Jesus said], \"And indeed, Allāh is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him. That is a straight path.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَرَبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَrَw", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَٱعْبُدُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُdُbْʿعٱَf", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صِرَٰطٌ", "translit": "ٌṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّسْتَقِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِqَtْsُّm", "root": "قوم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "فَٱخْتَلَفَ ٱلْأَحْزَابُ مِنۢ بَيْنِهِمْۖ فَوَيْلٌ لِّلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ مِن مَّشْهَدِ يَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍ ", "translation": "Then the factions differed [concerning Jesus] from among them, so woe to those who disbelieved - from the scene of a tremendous Day.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱخْتَلَفَ", "translit": "َfَlَtْhkٱَf", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَحْزَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَzْḥَaʾْlٱ", "root": "حزب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيْنِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَوَيْلٌ", "translit": "ٌlْyَwَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِّلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّشْهَدِ", "translit": "ِdَhْhsَّm", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَظِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "أَسْمِعْ بِهِمْ وَأَبْصِرْ يَوْمَ يَأْتُونَنَاۖ لَٰكِنِ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ ٱلْيَوْمَ فِى ضَلَٰلٍ مُّبِينٍ ", "translation": "How [clearly] they will hear and see the Day they come to Us, but the wrongdoers today are in clear error.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَسْمِعْ", "translit": "ْʿعِmْsَaʾ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَأَبْصِرْ", "translit": "ْrِṣْbَaʾَw", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَأْتُونَنَا", "translit": "āَnَnwُtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَٰكِنِ", "translit": "ِnِkَٰl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُّبِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "وَأَنذِرْهُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْحَسْرَةِ إِذْ قُضِىَ ٱلْأَمْرُ وَهُمْ فِى غَفْلَةٍ وَهُمْ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "And warn them, [O Muḥammad], of the Day of Regret, when the matter will be concluded;[1] and [yet], they are in [a state of] heedlessness, and they do not believe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنذِرْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْrِhdnَaʾَw", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَسْرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrْsَḥْlٱ", "root": "حسر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قُضِىَ", "translit": "َàِḍُq", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "غَفْلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlْfَغ", "root": "غفل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "إِنَّا نَحْنُ نَرِثُ ٱلْأَرْضَ وَمَنْ عَلَيْهَا وَإِلَيْنَا يُرْجَعُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, it is We who will inherit the earth and whoever is on it, and to Us they will be returned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَرِثُ", "translit": "ُhtِrَn", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُرْجَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَjgْrُy", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "وَٱذْكُرْ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ إِبْرَٰهِيمَۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ صِدِّيقًا نَّبِيًّا ", "translation": "And mention in the Book [the story of] Abraham. Indeed, he was a man of truth and a prophet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱذْكُرْ", "translit": "ْrُkْhdٱَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صِدِّيقًا", "translit": "āًqyِّdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَّبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَّn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "إِذْ قَالَ لِأَبِيهِ يَٰٓأَبَتِ لِمَ تَعْبُدُ مَا لَا يَسْمَعُ وَلَا يُبْصِرُ وَلَا يُغْنِى عَنكَ شَيْـًٔا ", "translation": "[Mention] when he said to his father, \"O my father, why do you worship that which does not hear and does not see and will not benefit you at all?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِأَبِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِbَaʾِl", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ", "translit": "ِtَbَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِمَ", "translit": "َmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُ", "translit": "ُdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُبْصِرُ", "translit": "ُrِṣْbُy", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُغْنِى", "translit": "àِnْغُy", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَنكَ", "translit": "َknَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ إِنِّى قَدْ جَآءَنِى مِنَ ٱلْعِلْمِ مَا لَمْ يَأْتِكَ فَٱتَّبِعْنِىٓ أَهْدِكَ صِرَٰطًا سَوِيًّا ", "translation": "O my father, indeed there has come to me of knowledge that which has not come to you, so follow me; I will guide you to an even path.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ", "translit": "ِtَbَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَآءَنِى", "translit": "àِnَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمِ", "translit": "ِmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَأْتِكَ", "translit": "َkِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَٱتَّبِعْنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnْʿعِbَّtٱَf", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَهْدِكَ", "translit": "َkِdْhَaʾ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "صِرَٰطًا", "translit": "āًṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَوِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِwَs", "root": "سوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ لَا تَعْبُدِ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَۖ إِنَّ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ كَانَ لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ عَصِيًّا ", "translation": "O my father, do not worship [i.e., obey] Satan. Indeed Satan has ever been, to the Most Merciful, disobedient.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ", "translit": "ِtَbَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَعْبُدِ", "translit": "ِdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlِl", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَصِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِṣَʿع", "root": "عصي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ إِنِّىٓ أَخَافُ أَن يَمَسَّكَ عَذَابٌ مِّنَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ فَتَكُونَ لِلشَّيْطَٰنِ وَلِيًّا ", "translation": "O my father, indeed I fear that there will touch you a punishment from the Most Merciful so you would be to Satan a companion [in Hellfire].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَبَتِ", "translit": "ِtَbَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَخَافُ", "translit": "ُfāَhkَaʾ", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَمَسَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّsَmَy", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَتَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَtَf", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِلشَّيْطَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰṭْyَّhslِl", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlَw", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "قَالَ أَرَاغِبٌ أَنتَ عَنْ ءَالِهَتِى يَٰٓإِبْرَٰهِيمُۖ لَئِن لَّمْ تَنتَهِ لَأَرْجُمَنَّكَۖ وَٱهْجُرْنِى مَلِيًّا ", "translation": "[His father] said, \"Have you no desire for my gods, O Abraham? If you do not desist, I will surely stone you, so avoid me a prolonged time.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرَاغِبٌ", "translit": "ٌbِغāَrَaʾ", "root": "رغب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَنْ", "translit": "ْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَالِهَتِى", "translit": "àِtَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَٰٓإِبْرَٰهِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِhَٰrْbِiʾَٰٓy", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَنتَهِ", "translit": "ِhَtnَt", "root": "نهي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَأَرْجُمَنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnَmُjgْrَaʾَl", "root": "رجم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱهْجُرْنِى", "translit": "àِnْrُjgْhٱَw", "root": "هجر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlَm", "root": "ملو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "قَالَ سَلَٰمٌ عَلَيْكَۖ سَأَسْتَغْفِرُ لَكَ رَبِّىٓۖ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ بِى حَفِيًّا ", "translation": "[Abraham] said, \"Peace [i.e., safety] will be upon you.[1] I will ask forgiveness for you of my Lord. Indeed, He is ever gracious to me.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَلَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰlَs", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سَأَسْتَغْفِرُ", "translit": "ُrِfْغَtْsَaʾَs", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِى", "translit": "àِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَفِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِfَḥ", "root": "حفو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "وَأَعْتَزِلُكُمْ وَمَا تَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ وَأَدْعُوا۟ رَبِّى عَسَىٰٓ أَلَّآ أَكُونَ بِدُعَآءِ رَبِّى شَقِيًّا ", "translation": "And I will leave you and those you invoke other than Allāh and will invoke[1] my Lord. I expect that I will not be in invocation to my Lord unhappy [i.e., disappointed].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَعْتَزِلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlِzَtْʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عزل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعْdَt", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdَaʾَw", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَaʾ", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِدُعَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَʿعُdِb", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "شَقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqَhs", "root": "شقو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "فَلَمَّا ٱعْتَزَلَهُمْ وَمَا يَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ وَهَبْنَا لَهُۥٓ إِسْحَٰقَ وَيَعْقُوبَۖ وَكُلًّا جَعَلْنَا نَبِيًّا ", "translation": "So when he had left them and those they worshipped other than Allāh, We gave him Isaac and Jacob, and each [of them] We made a prophet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱعْتَزَلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَzَtْʿعٱ", "root": "عزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْʿعَy", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَهَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَhَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِسْحَٰقَ", "translit": "َqَٰḥْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَيَعْقُوبَ", "translit": "َbwُqْʿعَyَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَكُلًّا", "translit": "āًّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "نَبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "وَوَهَبْنَا لَهُم مِّن رَّحْمَتِنَا وَجَعَلْنَا لَهُمْ لِسَانَ صِدْقٍ عَلِيًّا ", "translation": "And We gave them of Our mercy, and We made for them a mention [i.e., reputation] of high honor.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوَهَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَhَwَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَّحْمَتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَmْḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِسَانَ", "translit": "َnāَsِl", "root": "لسن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صِدْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlَʿع", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "وَٱذْكُرْ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ مُوسَىٰٓۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ مُخْلَصًا وَكَانَ رَسُولًا نَّبِيًّا ", "translation": "And mention in the Book, Moses. Indeed, he was chosen, and he was a messenger and a prophet.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱذْكُرْ", "translit": "ْrُkْhdٱَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُخْلَصًا", "translit": "āًṣَlْhkُm", "root": "خلص"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "نَّبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَّn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "وَنَٰدَيْنَٰهُ مِن جَانِبِ ٱلطُّورِ ٱلْأَيْمَنِ وَقَرَّبْنَٰهُ نَجِيًّا ", "translation": "And We called him from the side of the mount[1] at [his] right and brought him near, confiding [to him].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَٰدَيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَdَٰnَw", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَانِبِ", "translit": "ِbِnāَjg", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلطُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّṭlٱ", "root": "طور"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَيْمَنِ", "translit": "ِnَmْyَaʾْlٱ", "root": "يمن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَقَرَّبْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْbَّrَqَw", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نَجِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِjgَn", "root": "نجو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "وَوَهَبْنَا لَهُۥ مِن رَّحْمَتِنَآ أَخَاهُ هَٰرُونَ نَبِيًّا ", "translation": "And We gave him out of Our mercy his brother Aaron as a prophet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوَهَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَhَwَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَّحْمَتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَmْḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَخَاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نَبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "وَٱذْكُرْ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ إِسْمَٰعِيلَۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ صَادِقَ ٱلْوَعْدِ وَكَانَ رَسُولًا نَّبِيًّا ", "translation": "And mention in the Book, Ishmael. Indeed, he was true to his promise, and he was a messenger and a prophet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱذْكُرْ", "translit": "ْrُkْhdٱَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِسْمَٰعِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِʿعَٰmْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صَادِقَ", "translit": "َqِdāَṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْوَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَwْlٱ", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نَّبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَّn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "وَكَانَ يَأْمُرُ أَهْلَهُۥ بِٱلصَّلَوٰةِ وَٱلزَّكَوٰةِ وَكَانَ عِندَ رَبِّهِۦ مَرْضِيًّا ", "translation": "And he used to enjoin on his people prayer and zakāh and was to his Lord pleasing [i.e., accepted by Him].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَأْمُرُ", "translit": "ُrُmْaʾَy", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَهْلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlٱِb", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلزَّكَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَkَّzlٱَw", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَرْضِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِḍْrَm", "root": "رضو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "وَٱذْكُرْ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ إِدْرِيسَۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ صِدِّيقًا نَّبِيًّا ", "translation": "And mention in the Book, Idrees. Indeed, he was a man of truth and a prophet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱذْكُرْ", "translit": "ْrُkْhdٱَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِدْرِيسَ", "translit": "َsyِrْdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صِدِّيقًا", "translit": "āًqyِّdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَّبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَّn", "root": "نبا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "وَرَفَعْنَٰهُ مَكَانًا عَلِيًّا ", "translation": "And We raised him to a high station.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَرَفَعْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْʿعَfَrَw", "root": "رفع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَكَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَkَm", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlَʿع", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَيْهِم مِّنَ ٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ مِن ذُرِّيَّةِ ءَادَمَ وَمِمَّنْ حَمَلْنَا مَعَ نُوحٍ وَمِن ذُرِّيَّةِ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ وَإِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ وَمِمَّنْ هَدَيْنَا وَٱجْتَبَيْنَآۚ إِذَا تُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْهِمْ ءَايَٰتُ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ خَرُّوا۟ سُجَّدًا وَبُكِيًّا۩ ", "translation": "Those were the ones upon whom Allāh bestowed favor from among the prophets of the descendants of Adam and of those We carried [in the ship] with Noah, and of the descendants of Abraham and Israel [i.e., Jacob], and of those whom We guided and chose. When the verses of the Most Merciful were recited to them, they fell in prostration and weeping.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنْعَمَ", "translit": "َmَʿعْnَaʾ", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ", "translit": "َnۦِّyِbَّnlٱ", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ءَادَمَ", "translit": "َmَdāَء", "root": "ادم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمِمَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّmِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "حَمَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَmَḥ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَمِن", "translit": "nِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَإِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَمِمَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّmِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "هَدَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَdَh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَٱجْتَبَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَbَtْjgٱَw", "root": "جبي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "تُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُt", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "خَرُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّrَhk", "root": "خرر"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "سُجَّدًا", "translit": "āًdَّjgُs", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "وَبُكِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِkُbَw", "root": "بكي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "فَخَلَفَ مِنۢ بَعْدِهِمْ خَلْفٌ أَضَاعُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَٱتَّبَعُوا۟ ٱلشَّهَوَٰتِۖ فَسَوْفَ يَلْقَوْنَ غَيًّا ", "translation": "But there came after them successors [i.e., later generations] who neglected prayer and pursued desires; so they are going to meet evil[1] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَخَلَفَ", "translit": "َfَlَhkَf", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَلْفٌ", "translit": "ٌfْlَhk", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَضَاعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعāَḍَaʾ", "root": "ضيع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱتَّبَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَbَّtٱَw", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلشَّهَوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَhَّhslٱ", "root": "شهو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَسَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَsَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَلْقَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَqْlَy", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "غَيًّا", "translit": "āًّyَغ", "root": "غوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "إِلَّا مَن تَابَ وَءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًا فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ يَدْخُلُونَ ٱلْجَنَّةَ وَلَا يُظْلَمُونَ شَيْـًٔا ", "translation": "Except those who repent, believe and do righteousness; for those will enter Paradise and will not be wronged at all.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَابَ", "translit": "َbāَt", "root": "توب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَءَامَنَ", "translit": "َnَmāَءَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَعَمِلَ", "translit": "َlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "صَٰلِحًا", "translit": "āًḥِlَٰṣ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَدْخُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُhkْdَy", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُظْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْẓُy", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "جَنَّٰتِ عَدْنٍ ٱلَّتِى وَعَدَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ عِبَادَهُۥ بِٱلْغَيْبِۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ وَعْدُهُۥ مَأْتِيًّا ", "translation": "[Therein are] gardens of perpetual residence which the Most Merciful has promised His servants in the unseen. Indeed, His promise has ever been eminent.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَدْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْdَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَدَ", "translit": "َdَʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عِبَادَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱِb", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَعْدُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَأْتِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِtْaʾَm", "root": "اتي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "لَّا يَسْمَعُونَ فِيهَا لَغْوًا إِلَّا سَلَٰمًاۖ وَلَهُمْ رِزْقُهُمْ فِيهَا بُكْرَةً وَعَشِيًّا ", "translation": "They will not hear therein any ill speech - only [greetings of] peace - and they will have their provision therein, morning and afternoon.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَغْوًا", "translit": "āًwْغَl", "root": "لغو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَلَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰlَs", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رِزْقُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُqْzِr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بُكْرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrْkُb", "root": "بكر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَعَشِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِhsَʿعَw", "root": "عشو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 63, "text": "تِلْكَ ٱلْجَنَّةُ ٱلَّتِى نُورِثُ مِنْ عِبَادِنَا مَن كَانَ تَقِيًّا ", "translation": "That is Paradise, which We give as inheritance to those of Our servants who were fearing of Allāh.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تِلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِt", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةُ", "translit": "ُẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نُورِثُ", "translit": "ُhtِrwُn", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عِبَادِنَا", "translit": "āَnِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَقِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِqَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 64, "text": "وَمَا نَتَنَزَّلُ إِلَّا بِأَمْرِ رَبِّكَۖ لَهُۥ مَا بَيْنَ أَيْدِينَا وَمَا خَلْفَنَا وَمَا بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَۚ وَمَا كَانَ رَبُّكَ نَسِيًّا ", "translation": "[Gabriel said],[1] \"And we [angels] descend not except by the order of your Lord. To Him belongs that before us and that behind us and what is in between. And never is your Lord forgetful -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَتَنَزَّلُ", "translit": "ُlَّzَnَtَn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِأَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾِb", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَيْدِينَا", "translit": "āَnyِdْyَaʾ", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَلْفَنَا", "translit": "āَnَfْlَhk", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "رَبُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "نَسِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِsَn", "root": "نسي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 65, "text": "رَّبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا فَٱعْبُدْهُ وَٱصْطَبِرْ لِعِبَٰدَتِهِۦۚ هَلْ تَعْلَمُ لَهُۥ سَمِيًّا ", "translation": "Lord of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them - so worship Him and have patience for His worship. Do you know of any similarity to Him?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَّبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٱعْبُدْهُ", "translit": "ُhْdُbْʿعٱَf", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱصْطَبِرْ", "translit": "ْrِbَṭْṣٱَw", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِعِبَٰدَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَdَٰbِʿعِl", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سَمِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِmَs", "root": "سمو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 66, "text": "وَيَقُولُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ أَءِذَا مَا مِتُّ لَسَوْفَ أُخْرَجُ حَيًّا ", "translation": "And man [i.e., the disbeliever] says, \"When I have died, am I going to be brought forth alive?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَءِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِتُّ", "translit": "ُّtِm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَسَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَsَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أُخْرَجُ", "translit": "ُjgَrْhkُaʾ", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "حَيًّا", "translit": "āًّyَḥ", "root": "حيي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 67, "text": "أَوَلَا يَذْكُرُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ أَنَّا خَلَقْنَٰهُ مِن قَبْلُ وَلَمْ يَكُ شَيْـًٔا ", "translation": "Does man not remember that We created him before, while he was nothing?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَا", "translit": "āَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَذْكُرُ", "translit": "ُrُkْhdَy", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَكُ", "translit": "ُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 68, "text": "فَوَرَبِّكَ لَنَحْشُرَنَّهُمْ وَٱلشَّيَٰطِينَ ثُمَّ لَنُحْضِرَنَّهُمْ حَوْلَ جَهَنَّمَ جِثِيًّا ", "translation": "So by your Lord, We will surely gather them and the devils; then We will bring them to be present around Hell upon their knees.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَوَرَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَrَwَf", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَنَحْشُرَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَrُhsْḥَnَl", "root": "حشر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّيَٰطِينَ", "translit": "َnyِṭَٰyَّhslٱَw", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَنُحْضِرَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَrِḍْḥُnَl", "root": "حضر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "حَوْلَ", "translit": "َlْwَḥ", "root": "حول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جِثِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِhtِjg", "root": "جثو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 69, "text": "ثُمَّ لَنَنزِعَنَّ مِن كُلِّ شِيعَةٍ أَيُّهُمْ أَشَدُّ عَلَى ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ عِتِيًّا ", "translation": "Then We will surely extract from every sect those of them who were worst against the Most Merciful in insolence.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَنَنزِعَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَʿعِznَnَl", "root": "نزع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شِيعَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَʿعyِhs", "root": "شيع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَيُّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُّyَaʾ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَشَدُّ", "translit": "ُّdَhsَaʾ", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عِتِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِtِʿع", "root": "عتو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 70, "text": "ثُمَّ لَنَحْنُ أَعْلَمُ بِٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ أَوْلَىٰ بِهَا صِلِيًّا ", "translation": "Then, surely it is We who are most knowing of those most worthy of burning therein.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَنَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صِلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlِṣ", "root": "صلي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 71, "text": "وَإِن مِّنكُمْ إِلَّا وَارِدُهَاۚ كَانَ عَلَىٰ رَبِّكَ حَتْمًا مَّقْضِيًّا ", "translation": "And there is none of you except he will come to it.[1] This is upon your Lord an inevitability decreed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُknِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَارِدُهَا", "translit": "āَhُdِrāَw", "root": "ورد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "حَتْمًا", "translit": "āًmْtَḥ", "root": "حتم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّقْضِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِḍْqَّm", "root": "قضي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 72, "text": "ثُمَّ نُنَجِّى ٱلَّذِينَ ٱتَّقَوا۟ وَّنَذَرُ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ فِيهَا جِثِيًّا ", "translation": "Then We will save those who feared Allāh and leave the wrongdoers within it, on their knees.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُنَجِّى", "translit": "àِّjgَnُn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱتَّقَوا۟", "translit": "۟āwَqَّtٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَّنَذَرُ", "translit": "ُrَhdَnَّw", "root": "وذر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جِثِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِhtِjg", "root": "جثو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 73, "text": "وَإِذَا تُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْهِمْ ءَايَٰتُنَا بَيِّنَٰتٍ قَالَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ لِلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ أَىُّ ٱلْفَرِيقَيْنِ خَيْرٌ مَّقَامًا وَأَحْسَنُ نَدِيًّا ", "translation": "And when Our verses are recited to them as clear evidences, those who disbelieve say to those who believe, \"Which of [our] two parties is best in position and best in association?\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُt", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتُنَا", "translit": "āَnُtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيِّنَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰnِّyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَىُّ", "translit": "ُّàَaʾ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْفَرِيقَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَqyِrَfْlٱ", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَّقَامًا", "translit": "āًmāَqَّm", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَأَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾَw", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "نَدِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِdَn", "root": "ندي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 74, "text": "وَكَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا قَبْلَهُم مِّن قَرْنٍ هُمْ أَحْسَنُ أَثَٰثًا وَرِءْيًا ", "translation": "And how many a generation have We destroyed before them who were better in possessions and [outward] appearance?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَمْ", "translit": "ْmَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَبْلَهُم", "translit": "mُhَlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْrَq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَثَٰثًا", "translit": "āًhtَٰhtَaʾ", "root": "اثث"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَرِءْيًا", "translit": "āًyْءِrَw", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 75, "text": "قُلْ مَن كَانَ فِى ٱلضَّلَٰلَةِ فَلْيَمْدُدْ لَهُ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ مَدًّاۚ حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا رَأَوْا۟ مَا يُوعَدُونَ إِمَّا ٱلْعَذَابَ وَإِمَّا ٱلسَّاعَةَ فَسَيَعْلَمُونَ مَنْ هُوَ شَرٌّ مَّكَانًا وَأَضْعَفُ جُندًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Whoever is in error - let the Most Merciful extend for him an extension [in wealth and time] until, when they see that which they were promised - either punishment [in this world] or the Hour [of resurrection] - they will come to know who is worst in position and weaker in soldiers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلضَّلَٰلَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَlَٰlَّḍlٱ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَلْيَمْدُدْ", "translit": "ْdُdْmَyْlَf", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُ", "translit": "ُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَدًّا", "translit": "āًّdَm", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَأَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَaʾَr", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُوعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعwُy", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابَ", "translit": "َbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَإِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةَ", "translit": "َẗَʿعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فَسَيَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَyَsَf", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "شَرٌّ", "translit": "ٌّrَhs", "root": "شرر"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مَّكَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَkَّm", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَأَضْعَفُ", "translit": "ُfَʿعْḍَaʾَw", "root": "ضعف"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "جُندًا", "translit": "āًdnُjg", "root": "جند"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 76, "text": "وَيَزِيدُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱهْتَدَوْا۟ هُدًىۗ وَٱلْبَٰقِيَٰتُ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ خَيْرٌ عِندَ رَبِّكَ ثَوَابًا وَخَيْرٌ مَّرَدًّا ", "translation": "And Allāh increases those who were guided, in guidance, and the enduring good deeds are better to your Lord[1] for reward and better for recourse.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَزِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِzَyَw", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱهْتَدَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَdَtْhٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُدًى", "translit": "àًdُh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْبَٰقِيَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰyِqَٰbْlٱَw", "root": "بقي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ثَوَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَwَht", "root": "ثوب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَخَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhkَw", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَّرَدًّا", "translit": "āًّdَrَّm", "root": "ردد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 77, "text": "أَفَرَءَيْتَ ٱلَّذِى كَفَرَ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا وَقَالَ لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا ", "translation": "Then, have you seen he who disbelieved in Our verses and said, \"I will surely be given wealth and children [in the next life]\"?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَرَءَيْتَ", "translit": "َtْyَءَrَfَaʾ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرَ", "translit": "َrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَأُوتَيَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَyَtwُaʾَl", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَالًا", "translit": "āًlāَm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَوَلَدًا", "translit": "āًdَlَwَw", "root": "ولد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 78, "text": "أَطَّلَعَ ٱلْغَيْبَ أَمِ ٱتَّخَذَ عِندَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ عَهْدًا ", "translation": "Has he looked into the unseen, or has he taken from the Most Merciful a promise?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَطَّلَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَlَّṭَaʾ", "root": "طلع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْغَيْبَ", "translit": "َbْyَغْlٱ", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَمِ", "translit": "ِmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذَ", "translit": "َhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَهْدًا", "translit": "āًdْhَʿع", "root": "عهد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 79, "text": "كَلَّاۚ سَنَكْتُبُ مَا يَقُولُ وَنَمُدُّ لَهُۥ مِنَ ٱلْعَذَابِ مَدًّا ", "translation": "No! We will record what he says and extend [i.e., increase] for him from the punishment extensively.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَنَكْتُبُ", "translit": "ُbُtْkَnَs", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَنَمُدُّ", "translit": "ُّdُmَnَw", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَدًّا", "translit": "āًّdَm", "root": "مدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 80, "text": "وَنَرِثُهُۥ مَا يَقُولُ وَيَأْتِينَا فَرْدًا ", "translation": "And We will inherit him [in] what he mentions,[1] and he will come to Us alone.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَرِثُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُhtِrَnَw", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَأْتِينَا", "translit": "āَnyِtْaʾَyَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَرْدًا", "translit": "āًdْrَf", "root": "فرد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 81, "text": "وَٱتَّخَذُوا۟ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ ءَالِهَةً لِّيَكُونُوا۟ لَهُمْ عِزًّا ", "translation": "And they have taken besides Allāh [false] deities that they would be for them [a source of] honor.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱتَّخَذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdَhkَّtٱَw", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةً", "translit": "ًẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِّيَكُونُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnwُkَyِّl", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عِزًّا", "translit": "āًّzِʿع", "root": "عزز"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 82, "text": "كَلَّاۚ سَيَكْفُرُونَ بِعِبَادَتِهِمْ وَيَكُونُونَ عَلَيْهِمْ ضِدًّا ", "translation": "No! They [i.e., those \"gods\"] will deny their worship of them and will be against them opponents [on the Day of Judgement].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَيَكْفُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُfْkَyَs", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِعِبَادَتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtَdāَbِʿعِb", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَكُونُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnwُkَyَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ضِدًّا", "translit": "āًّdِḍ", "root": "ضدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 83, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّآ أَرْسَلْنَا ٱلشَّيَٰطِينَ عَلَى ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ تَؤُزُّهُمْ أَزًّا ", "translation": "Do you not see that We have sent the devils upon the disbelievers, inciting them [to evil] with [constant] incitement?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيَٰطِينَ", "translit": "َnyِṭَٰyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَؤُزُّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُّzُwʾَt", "root": "ازز"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَزًّا", "translit": "āًّzَaʾ", "root": "ازز"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 84, "text": "فَلَا تَعْجَلْ عَلَيْهِمْۖ إِنَّمَا نَعُدُّ لَهُمْ عَدًّا ", "translation": "So be not impatient over them. We only count out [i.e., allow] to them a [limited] number.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَعْجَلْ", "translit": "ْlَjgْʿعَt", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَعُدُّ", "translit": "ُّdُʿعَn", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَدًّا", "translit": "āًّdَʿع", "root": "عدد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 85, "text": "يَوْمَ نَحْشُرُ ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ إِلَى ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ وَفْدًا ", "translation": "On the Day We will gather the righteous to the Most Merciful as a delegation", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَحْشُرُ", "translit": "ُrُhsْḥَn", "root": "حشر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqَّtُmْlٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَفْدًا", "translit": "āًdْfَw", "root": "وفد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 86, "text": "وَنَسُوقُ ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ إِلَىٰ جَهَنَّمَ وِرْدًا ", "translation": "And will drive the criminals to Hell in thirst", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَسُوقُ", "translit": "ُqwُsَnَw", "root": "سوق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِrْjgُmْlٱ", "root": "جرم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وِرْدًا", "translit": "āًdْrِw", "root": "ورد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 87, "text": "لَّا يَمْلِكُونَ ٱلشَّفَٰعَةَ إِلَّا مَنِ ٱتَّخَذَ عِندَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ عَهْدًا ", "translation": "None will have [power of] intercession except he who had taken from the Most Merciful a covenant.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَمْلِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِlْmَy", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلشَّفَٰعَةَ", "translit": "َẗَʿعَٰfَّhslٱ", "root": "شفع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَنِ", "translit": "ِnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذَ", "translit": "َhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَهْدًا", "translit": "āًdْhَʿع", "root": "عهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 88, "text": "وَقَالُوا۟ ٱتَّخَذَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ وَلَدًا ", "translation": "And they say, \"The Most Merciful has taken [for Himself] a son.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذَ", "translit": "َhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلَدًا", "translit": "āًdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 89, "text": "لَّقَدْ جِئْتُمْ شَيْـًٔا إِدًّا ", "translation": "You have done an atrocious thing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جِئْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْyʾِjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِدًّا", "translit": "āًّdِiʾ", "root": "ادد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 90, "text": "تَكَادُ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ يَتَفَطَّرْنَ مِنْهُ وَتَنشَقُّ ٱلْأَرْضُ وَتَخِرُّ ٱلْجِبَالُ هَدًّا ", "translation": "The heavens almost rupture therefrom and the earth splits open and the mountains collapse in devastation", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تَكَادُ", "translit": "ُdāَkَt", "root": "كود"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَتَفَطَّرْنَ", "translit": "َnْrَّṭَfَtَy", "root": "فطر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَتَنشَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَhsnَtَw", "root": "شقق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَتَخِرُّ", "translit": "ُّrِhkَtَw", "root": "خرر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْجِبَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَbِjgْlٱ", "root": "جبل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَدًّا", "translit": "āًّdَh", "root": "هدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 91, "text": "أَن دَعَوْا۟ لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ وَلَدًا ", "translation": "That they attribute to the Most Merciful a son.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "دَعَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَʿعَd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlِl", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلَدًا", "translit": "āًdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 92, "text": "وَمَا يَنۢبَغِى لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ أَن يَتَّخِذَ وَلَدًا ", "translation": "And it is not appropriate for the Most Merciful that He should take a son.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَنۢبَغِى", "translit": "àِغَbۢnَy", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlِl", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَتَّخِذَ", "translit": "َhdِhkَّtَy", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَدًا", "translit": "āًdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 93, "text": "إِن كُلُّ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ إِلَّآ ءَاتِى ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ عَبْدًا ", "translation": "There is no one in the heavens and earth but that he comes to the Most Merciful as a servant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ءَاتِى", "translit": "àِtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَبْدًا", "translit": "āًdْbَʿع", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 94, "text": "لَّقَدْ أَحْصَىٰهُمْ وَعَدَّهُمْ عَدًّا ", "translation": "He has enumerated them and counted them a [full] counting.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَحْصَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَṣْḥَaʾ", "root": "حصي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَدَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّdَʿعَw", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَدًّا", "translit": "āًّdَʿع", "root": "عدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 95, "text": "وَكُلُّهُمْ ءَاتِيهِ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ فَرْدًا ", "translation": "And all of them are coming to Him on the Day of Resurrection alone.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكُلُّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَرْدًا", "translit": "āًdْrَf", "root": "فرد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 96, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ سَيَجْعَلُ لَهُمُ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ وُدًّا ", "translation": "Indeed, those who have believed and done righteous deeds - the Most Merciful will appoint for them affection.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَيَجْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَʿعْjgَyَs", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وُدًّا", "translit": "āًّdُw", "root": "ودد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 97, "text": "فَإِنَّمَا يَسَّرْنَٰهُ بِلِسَانِكَ لِتُبَشِّرَ بِهِ ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ وَتُنذِرَ بِهِۦ قَوْمًا لُّدًّا ", "translation": "So, [O Muḥammad], We have only made it [i.e., the Qur’ān] easy in your tongue [i.e., the Arabic language] that you may give good tidings thereby to the righteous and warn thereby a hostile people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسَّرْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْrَّsَy", "root": "يسر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِلِسَانِكَ", "translit": "َkِnāَsِlِb", "root": "لسن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِتُبَشِّرَ", "translit": "َrِّhsَbُtِl", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqَّtُmْlٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَتُنذِرَ", "translit": "َrِhdnُtَw", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لُّدًّا", "translit": "āًّdُّl", "root": "لدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 98, "text": "وَكَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا قَبْلَهُم مِّن قَرْنٍ هَلْ تُحِسُّ مِنْهُم مِّنْ أَحَدٍ أَوْ تَسْمَعُ لَهُمْ رِكْزًۢا ", "translation": "And how many have We destroyed before them of generations? Do you perceive of them anyone or hear from them a sound?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَمْ", "translit": "ْmَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَبْلَهُم", "translit": "mُhَlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْrَq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُحِسُّ", "translit": "ُّsِḥُt", "root": "حسس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَحَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "تَسْمَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَmْsَt", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رِكْزًۢا", "translit": "āًۢzْkِr", "root": "ركز"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"3": [{"arabic": "بها} (11) أي بدعائك وقد أثنى الله عز وجل على نبيه زكرياء عليه السلام حيث قال {إذ نادى ربه نداء خفيا} وقال عز وجل {ادعوا ربكم تضرعا وخفية} الخامس أن لا يتكلف السجع في الدعاء فإن حال الداعي ينبغي أن يكون حال متضرع والتكلف لا يناسبه قال صلى الله عليه وسلم سيكون قوم يعتدون في الدعاء (12) وقد قال عز وجل {ادعوا ربكم تضرعا وخفية إنه} {لا يحب المعتدين} قيل معناه التكلف للأسجاع والأولى أن لا يجاوز الدعوات المأثورة فإنه قد يعتدي في دعائه فيسأل ما لا تقتضيه مصلحته فما كل أحد يحسن الدعاء ولذلك روي عن معاذ رضي الله عنه أن العلماء", "english": "[Root: دعو]\n  • Lisan al-Arab: \"الدعاء: الرغبة إلى الله تعالى، والابتهال في المسألة. والدعوة: الكلمة التي يدعو بها. وأصل الدعاء: طلب الإقبال من الغير.\"\n  • Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدعاء: ضربان: أحدهما دعاء العبادة، والآخر دعاء المسألة. فدعاء العبادة: هو توجه العبد إلى الله تعالى بالطاعة. ودعاء المسألة: طلب الحاجة من الله تعالى. قال تعالى: {وقال ربكم ادعوني أستجب لكم}.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"دعا فلان ربه: ابتهل إليه بالسؤال. ودعا الله: عبده. ومنه قوله تعالى: {إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي} أي عن دعائي.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"دعا عليه: سأل الله أن يصيبه بمكروه. ودعا له: سأل الله أن يحسن إليه. ومن المجاز: دعا الأمر إلى كذا، أي أدى إليه.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 242", "section_title_en": "Section 242", "section_index": 242, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "12": [{"arabic": "الثالث حضور القلب وترك حديث النفس قيل في تفسير {يا يحيى خذ الكتاب بقوة} أي بجد واجتهاد وأخذه بالجد أن يكون متجردا له عند قراءته منصرف الهمة إليه عن غيره وقيل لبعضهم إذا قرأت القرآن تحدث نفسك بشيء فقال أو شيء أحب إلي من القران حتى أحدث به نفسي وكان بعض السلف إذا قرأ آية لم يكن قلبه فيها أعادها ثانية وهذه الصفة تتولد عما قبلها من التعظيم فإن المعظم للكلام الذي يتلوه يستبشر به ويستأنس ولا يغفل عنه ففي القرآن ما يستأنس به القلب إن كان التالي أهلا له فكيف يطلب الأنس بالفكر في غيره وهو في متنزه ومتفرج", "english": "And one of the sages shone brilliantly in expressing the aspect of gentleness in conveying the meanings of speech — despite the exaltedness of its rank — to human understanding, and establishing it firmly despite the limitation of humanity's station. He struck for it a parable that fell short of nothing. That was when a certain sage was summoned by one of the kings to the law of the prophets — peace be upon them. The king asked him about matters, and he answered with what his understanding could not bear. The king said: \"What do you say of what the prophets bring when they claim that it is not the speech of men, but that it is the speech of Allah — mighty and majestic is He? How then can people bear to carry it?\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 226", "section_title_en": "The Ten Principles of Qur'anic Recitation — The Inward Realities of Recitation (Section 226)", "section_index": 226, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, understanding; the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one ascertains meanings; divine favor in bestowing understanding\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — distinguishes passive reception from active divine instruction\n- Root: عظم (ʿ-ẓ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bigness of bone; then metaphorically extended to all magnitude, sensory or intelligible\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"عظم الشيء أصله: كبر عظمه، ثم استعير لكل كبير\" — literal bone-magnitude extended metaphorically to majesty and grandeur\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لا يتعاظمني أي لا يعظم في عيني\" — what is too great for one's capacity to bear\n- Root: حكم (ḥ-k-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"حكم أصله: منع منعا لإصلاح\" — restraint for the sake of rectification; hence the rein (ḥikmah) of the beast\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أحكم الشيء فاستحكم\" — to make firm so that it becomes firm; the penetrating authority of wisdom\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the prepositional phrase (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the periodic suspension between the divine attribute and its creaturely vehicle, as in \"من كلام الله\" where the genitive chain marks ontological descent without essential change", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "فيها حبا وعنبا وقال عز وجل فأرسلنا إليها روحنا فتمثل لها بشرا سويا ثم قال تعالى فنفخنا فيها من روحنا وكان النافخ جبريل عليه السلام وكما قال تعالى فإذا قرأناه فاتبع قرآنه قيل في التفسير معناه إذا قرأه عليك جبريل وقال تعالى قاتلوهم يعذبهم الله بأيديكم فأضاف القتل إليهم والتعذيب إلى نفسه والتعذيب هو عين", "english": "Thus should you understand the issuance of determined things (al-maqdūrāt) from eternal power — even though the power is eternal (qadīmah) and the objects of power are originated (ḥādithah). And this is a knocking upon another door of another world among the worlds of unveilings (mukāshafāt). So let us leave all of that, for our purpose is only to alert to the path of unification (tawḥīd) in the act — for the agent in truth (al-fāʿil bi-al-ḥaqīqah) is One. He is the feared and the hoped, and upon Him is reliance and trust. And we have not been able to mention from the seas of tawḥīd save a drop from the ocean of the third station among the stations of tawḥīd. And exhausting that within the lifetime of Noah is impossible — like exhausting the water of the sea by taking droplets from it. And all of that is subsumed under the saying: \"There is no god but God\" (lā ilāha illā Allāh). How light is its burden upon the tongue, how easy is the belief in the meaning of its utterance upon the heart, and how precious is its reality and its kernel (lubbuhu) among the scholars firmly rooted in knowledge — so how [much more so] with others?", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1114", "section_title_en": "The Oneness of the Actor (Tawḥīd al-Fiʿl) — Reconciling Divine and Human Agency", "section_index": 1114, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes measure, decree, and capacity; al-Farāhīdī anchors it in the physical act of measuring or sizing, extended to divine determining.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-qudrah\" when ascribed to God negates incapacity (ʿajz) absolutely; when ascribed to man, it names a state (hayʾah) enabling a specific act — never absolute power, but restricted capacity over particular objects.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He is qādir, possessing power.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"faras baʿīd al-qadr\" (a far-striding horse) — power's \"reach\" extended figuratively; here, divine power's \"presence\" with its objects is literal for God, metaphorical for creatures.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth\": coming-to-be after non-existence; al-ḥadath: ritual impurity (as in this text's water analogy); al-muḥdath: the originated thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: the thing's being after it was not — whether accident or substance. Its iḥdāth (bringing-about) is exclusive to God.\" Creaturely acts are originated, but their origination is divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent, newly arisen. Metaphorical: \"ʿUmar was muḥaddath\" (inspired in judgment) — as if spoken to by unseen truth; here, the \"newly arisen\" (ḥādith) effect is tied to a condition (sharṭ), not a new power.\n- Root: ماء (m-ā-ʾ)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Water as the origin of life ({وجعلنا من الماء كل شيء حي}); its root is m-w-h, with hāʾ dropped and wāw shifted — a paradigm for how a thing's \"presence\" (mulāqāh) does not entail its \"effect\" (taʾthīr) absent a condition.\n  * Kitab al-Ayn: Water's essence is unchanged; the analogy hinges on its constancy.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-wajh\" is the face; then extended to the \"foremost\" or \"noblest\" part of any thing, and to the self (dhāt) in {ويبقى وجه ربك}. Here, \"washing the face\" is the specified condition (sharṭ) — the \"foremost\" ritual act — not the efficient cause of purification.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the bodily face. Metaphorical: \"wajh al-qawm\" (the leaders), \"wajh al-thawb\" (the surface of a garment) — the \"face\" as the operative locus; in the analogy, the face's washing is the trigger, not the agent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing principle: a dependent element (here, divine power's \"attachment\" to objects) can be separated from its governor by an intervening condition, just as grammatical separation is permissible when the intervening clause is complete; analogically, the \"link\" (irṭibāṭ) of power to its effect is mediated by conditions without altering the agent's nature.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "33": [{"arabic": "عليه السلام من المفضلين ولإدلاله سلم على نفسه فقال والسلام علي يوم ولدت ويوم أموت ويوم أبعث حيا وهذا انبساط منه لما شاهد من اللطف في مقام الأنس وأما يحيى بن زكريا عليه السلام فإنه أقيم مقام الهيبة والحياء فلم ينطق حتى أثنى عليه خالقه فقال وسلام عليه وانظر كيف احتمل لأخوة يوسف ما فعلوه بيوسف وقد قال بعض العلماء قد عددت من أول قوله تعالى إذ قالوا ليوسف وأخوه أحب إلى أبينا منا إلى رأس العشرين من إخباره تعالى عن زهدهم فيه نيفا وأربعين خطيئة بعضها أكبر من بعض وقد يجتمع في الكلمة الواحدة الثلاث والأربع فغفر لهم وعفا عنهم ولم يحتمل العزير في مسألة", "english": "As for Adam (peace be upon him), it was said concerning him: {«وعصى آدم ربه فغوى»} \"And Adam disobeyed his Lord, so he went astray\" (Q. 20:121), then {«اجتباه ربه فتاب عليه وهدى»} \"his Lord chose him, and turned to him in forgiveness, and guided him\" (Q. 20:122).", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1187", "section_title_en": "On the Varieties of Divine Proximity (Al-Inbisāṭ) and Awe (Al-Haybah) in the States of the Prophets and Saints", "section_index": 1187, "anchors": "- Root: بسط (B-S-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to spread out, extend, expand\" — both physically (basting a garment) and metaphorically.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"بسط الشيء: نشره وتوسيعه\" — used for expansion of mercy, favor, and the soul's comfort. In Kalam, it contrasts with *qabḍ* (contraction/withholding).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"بسط الثوب والفراش إذا نشره.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"بسطني ما بسطك\" (what pleases you pleases me) — hence *inbisāṭ* here denotes the soul's expansive intimacy with God, a spiritual state, not a physical act.\n- Root: أبلس (B-L-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"أبلس: انقطعت حجته ويئس\" — to be reduced to silence, to despair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبلاس: الحزن المعترض من شدة اليأس\" — the despair of Iblis is a theological state of being cut off from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: despair/silence. Metaphorical: Iblis (the proper name) is derived from this root — he is the one \"despairing\" of divine mercy.\n- Root: عله (not used; avoided per instruction) — *Note: The text's key term \"الإدلال\" (al-idlāl) is from root دلو (D-L-W).*\n- Root: دلو (D-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دلا الدلو: أخرجها من البئر\" — to draw water from a well; hence *iddalā'a* (إدلال) means to advance boldly, relying on a familiar bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not a primary Quranic term, but lexically implies confident approach based on prior intimacy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: lowering the bucket. Metaphorical: \"أدلّ عليه\" — to act with bold familiarity toward someone who loves you, trusting in their affection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Applied in the phrase \"فإنما هي عند ذوي الاعتبار من الأسماء\" (they are but names to those who reflect), restricting the ontological status of stories to mere signifiers for the discerning.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "39": [{"arabic": "تعالى وأنذرهم يوم الحسرة إذ قضي الأمر وهم في غفلة وهم لا يؤمنون", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "41": [{"arabic": "الصديق مشتق منه والله تعالى وصف الأنبياء به في معرض المدح والثناء فقال {واذكر في الكتاب إبراهيم إنه كان صديقا نبيا} وقال واذكر في الكتاب إسمعيل إنه كان صادق الوعد وكان رسولا نبيا وقال تعالى {واذكر في الكتاب إدريس إنه كان صديقا نبيا} وقال ابن عباس أربع من كن فيه فقد ربح الصدق والحياء وحسن الخلق والشكر وقال بشر ابن الحارث من عامل الله بالصدق استوحش من الناس وقال أبو عبد الله الرملي رأيت منصور الدينوري في المنام فقلت له ما فعل الله بك قال غفر لي ورحمني وأعطاني ما لم أؤمل فقلت له أحسن ما توجه العبد به إلى الله ماذا قال الصدق و", "english": "God Most High has said: {«رجال صدقوا ما عاهدوا الله عليه»} — \"Among the believers are men who have been true to the covenant they made with God\" (Al-Aḥzāb 33:23). And the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Verily, truthfulness leads to righteousness (al-birr), and righteousness leads to Paradise. And verily, a man continues to speak truthfully until he is written with God as a supremely veracious one (ṣiddīq). And verily, falsehood leads to wickedness (al-fujūr), and wickedness leads to the Fire. And verily, a man continues to lie until he is written with God as a liar (kadhdhāb).\" (1)", "book_title": "Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness", "section_title_ar": "Section 1226", "section_title_en": "The Book of Truthfulness (Ṣidq): Its Virtue and Reality — Section on the Virtue of Truthfulness", "section_index": 1226, "anchors": "- Root: صدق (Ṣ-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the firmness and soundness of a thing; a spear termed \"ṣadq\" is straight and rigid; truthfulness is speech that is firm, sound, and free of crookedness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — truthfulness and falsehood originate in speech, whether past or future, promise or otherwise; they occur by primary intent only in informative statements (khabar), not in other speech categories. Al-Raghib further notes the derived degrees: ṣidq (truthfulness), ṣiddīq (the supremely veracious — one whose truthfulness has become his very character), and ṣādiq al-waʿd (truthful in promise).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (literal): \"صدقته الحديث\" — to affirm someone's report as true. Majaz (metaphorical): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — a man truthful in his charge (i.e., steadfast and unfaltering in battle), and \"فرس ذو مصدق في الجري\" — a horse with genuine exertion in running. Thus ṣidq extends metaphorically to any state of firmness, steadfastness, and correspondence between inner conviction and outward action.\n- Root: بر (B-R-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Birr: expansive goodness, piety, and obedience; the \"barr\" (land) is so named for its expanse. It encompasses all acts of righteousness and kindness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-birr is the breadth of obedience to God through sincere intention and righteous action; it is the comprehensive term for all that God loves and approves.\n- Root: فجر (F-J-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Fujūr: the breaking forth and bursting open — the wickedness that breaks through the barrier of piety; the \"fājir\" is one who bursts out of obedience into transgression.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fujūr is the violation of the covenant of obedience, the breaking open of the seal of God-consciousness; it is the opposite of birr.\n- Root: كذب (K-DH-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Kidhb: falsehood — informing of a thing contrary to its reality; the root also carries the sense of deficiency and weakness (as in \"kadhaba\" — to fall short).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood is the counterpart of ṣidq — it is reporting what does not correspond to reality, whether in past, present, or future tense.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The grammatical structure \"يكتب عند الله صديقا\" (he is written with God as a ṣiddīq) involves the prepositional phrase \"عند الله\" interposed between the verb and its predicate accusative, a construction permissible when the interposed element is semantically integral to the judgment, as here where divine decree is the ground of the designation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "42": [{"arabic": "الأصنام جهلا وغيا فقال له لم تعبد ما لا يسمع ولا يبصر ولا يغني عنك شيئا ولو انقلب ذلك عليه في معبوده لأضحت حجته داحضة ودلالته ساقطة ولم يصدق قوله تعالى وتلك حجتنا آتيناها إبراهيم على قومه وكما عقل كونه فاعلا بلا جارحة وعالما بلا قلب ودماغ فليعقل كونه بصيرا بلا حدقة وسميعا بلا اذن إذ لا فرق بينهما الأصل السادس أنه سبحانه وتعالى متكلم بكلام وهو وصف قائم بذاته ليس بصوت ولا حرف بل لا يشبه كلامه كلام غيره كما لا يشبه وجوده وجود غيره والكلام بالحقيقة كلام النفس وإنما الأصوات قطعت حروفا للدلالات كما يدل عليها تارة بالحركات", "english": "[Root: عقل]\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is the faculty prepared for receiving knowledge, and the knowledge acquired through that faculty is also called intellect. This dual sense is crucial for understanding Ghazali's argument that true intellection ('aql) grasps realities beyond sensory perception.\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"عقل فلان بعد الصبا أي عرف الخطأ الذي كان عليه\" — To exercise intellect means to recognize error. Ghazali employs this to demand intellectual consistency from his opponents.\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — A palm tree that does not accept pollination is said metaphorically to \"not comprehend.\" This illustrates that 'aql involves receptivity to what is appropriate.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 91", "section_title_en": "Section 91", "section_index": 91, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "48": [{"arabic": "احتجوا بقوله تعالى حكاية عن إبراهيم عليه السلام {وأعتزلكم وما تدعون من دون الله وأدعو ربي} الآية ثم قال تعالى فلما اعتزلهم وما يعبدون من دون الله وهبنا له إسحق ويعقوب وكلا جعلنا نبيا إشارة إلى أن ذلك ببركة العزلة وهذا ضعيف لأن مخالطة الكفار لا فائدة فيها إلا دعوتهم الى الدين وعند اليأس من إجابتهم فلا وجه إلا هجرهم وإنما الكلام في مخالطة المسلمين وما فيها من البركة لما روي أنه قيل يا رسول الله الوضوء من جر مخمر أحب إليك أو من هذه المطاهر التي يتطهر منها الناس فقال بل من هذه المطاهر التماسا لبركة أيدي المسلمين (1) وروي أنه صل", "english": "They argued by the saying of Allah Almighty, narrating of Abraham, peace be upon him: {«وَأَعْتَزِلُكُمْ وَمَا تَدْعُونَ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَأَدْعُو رَبِّي»} [And I will withdraw from you and what you call upon besides Allah, and I will call upon my Lord] — to the end of the verse. Then Allah Almighty said: {«فَلَمَّا اعْتَزَلَهُمْ وَمَا يَعْبُدُونَ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَهَبْنَا لَهُ إِسْحَاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَكُلًّا جَعَلْنَا نَبِيًّا»} [So when he withdrew from them and what they worshipped besides Allah, We granted him Isaac and Jacob, and each We made a prophet] — indicating that this was by the blessing of seclusion.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Seclusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 446", "section_title_en": "Section 446 — On the Evidences for and Against Seclusion (Al-ʿUzlah) and the Refutation of Both Extremes", "section_index": 446, "anchors": "- Root: عزل (ʿ-z-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of separation, withdrawal, setting apart; al-iʿtizāl is to remove oneself from company or affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاعتزال: تجنب الشيء\" — seclusion is the shunning of a thing, whether by body or by heart, whether as an act of piety, innocence, or otherwise. It is used in the Qur'an for withdrawing from idolaters {«وَإِذَا اعْتَزَلْتُمُوهُمْ وَمَا يَعْبُدُونَ إِلَّا اللَّهَ»} and from women during menses {«فَاعْتَزِلُوا النِّسَاءَ»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): physical separation from a place or people. Metaphorical (majāz): withdrawal of the heart from worldly concerns or from the company of the corrupt.\n- Root: خلط (kh-l-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Mixing, blending, intermingling; a companion or partner is called khalīṭ because of shared association.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلط: هو الجمع بين أجزاء الشيئين فصاعدا\" — mixing is the joining of parts of two or more things; extended metaphorically to social mixing (mukhālaṭah) with people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mixing substances. Metaphorical: social intercourse, companionship, partnership — \"خالطت فلانا، وهو خليطي\" (I mixed with so-and-so, and he is my companion).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The particle innamā restricts predication to a single referent, as in the Prophet's ﷺ saying \"إنما العلم بالتعلم\" (Knowledge is only attained by learning), which confines the means of acquiring knowledge to learning alone.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Seclusion'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "49": [{"arabic": "دون الله وأدعو ربي} الآية ثم قال تعالى فلما اعتزلهم وما يعبدون من دون الله وهبنا له إسحق ويعقوب وكلا جعلنا نبيا إشارة إلى أن ذلك ببركة العزلة وهذا ضعيف لأن مخالطة الكفار لا فائدة فيها إلا دعوتهم الى الدين وعند اليأس من إجابتهم فلا وجه إلا هجرهم وإنما الكلام في مخالطة المسلمين وما فيها من البركة لما روي أنه قيل يا رسول الله الوضوء من جر مخمر أحب إليك أو من هذه المطاهر التي يتطهر منها الناس فقال بل من هذه المطاهر التماسا لبركة أيدي المسلمين (1) وروي أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم لما طاف بالبيت عدل الى زمزم ليشرب منها فإذا التمر المنقع في حياض الأدم وق", "english": "They argued by the saying of Allah Almighty, narrating of Abraham, peace be upon him: {«وَأَعْتَزِلُكُمْ وَمَا تَدْعُونَ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَأَدْعُو رَبِّي»} [And I will withdraw from you and what you call upon besides Allah, and I will call upon my Lord] — to the end of the verse. Then Allah Almighty said: {«فَلَمَّا اعْتَزَلَهُمْ وَمَا يَعْبُدُونَ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَهَبْنَا لَهُ إِسْحَاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَكُلًّا جَعَلْنَا نَبِيًّا»} [So when he withdrew from them and what they worshipped besides Allah, We granted him Isaac and Jacob, and each We made a prophet] — indicating that this was by the blessing of seclusion.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Seclusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 446", "section_title_en": "Section 446 — On the Evidences for and Against Seclusion (Al-ʿUzlah) and the Refutation of Both Extremes", "section_index": 446, "anchors": "- Root: عزل (ʿ-z-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of separation, withdrawal, setting apart; al-iʿtizāl is to remove oneself from company or affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاعتزال: تجنب الشيء\" — seclusion is the shunning of a thing, whether by body or by heart, whether as an act of piety, innocence, or otherwise. It is used in the Qur'an for withdrawing from idolaters {«وَإِذَا اعْتَزَلْتُمُوهُمْ وَمَا يَعْبُدُونَ إِلَّا اللَّهَ»} and from women during menses {«فَاعْتَزِلُوا النِّسَاءَ»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): physical separation from a place or people. Metaphorical (majāz): withdrawal of the heart from worldly concerns or from the company of the corrupt.\n- Root: خلط (kh-l-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Mixing, blending, intermingling; a companion or partner is called khalīṭ because of shared association.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلط: هو الجمع بين أجزاء الشيئين فصاعدا\" — mixing is the joining of parts of two or more things; extended metaphorically to social mixing (mukhālaṭah) with people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mixing substances. Metaphorical: social intercourse, companionship, partnership — \"خالطت فلانا، وهو خليطي\" (I mixed with so-and-so, and he is my companion).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The particle innamā restricts predication to a single referent, as in the Prophet's ﷺ saying \"إنما العلم بالتعلم\" (Knowledge is only attained by learning), which confines the means of acquiring knowledge to learning alone.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Seclusion'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "54": [{"arabic": "{واذكر في الكتاب إبراهيم إنه كان صديقا نبيا} وقال واذكر في الكتاب إسمعيل إنه كان صادق الوعد وكان رسولا نبيا وقال تعالى {واذكر في الكتاب إدريس إنه كان صديقا نبيا} وقال ابن عباس أربع من كن فيه فقد ربح الصدق والحياء وحسن الخلق والشكر وقال بشر ابن الحارث من عامل الله بالصدق استوحش من الناس وقال أبو عبد الله الرملي رأيت منصور الدينوري في المنام فقلت له ما فعل الله بك قال غفر لي ورحمني وأعطاني ما لم أؤمل فقلت له أحسن ما توجه العبد به إلى الله ماذا قال الصدق وأقبح ما توجه به الكذب وقال أبو سليمان اجعل الصدق مطيتك والح", "english": "God Most High has said: {«رجال صدقوا ما عاهدوا الله عليه»} — \"Among the believers are men who have been true to the covenant they made with God\" (Al-Aḥzāb 33:23). And the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Verily, truthfulness leads to righteousness (al-birr), and righteousness leads to Paradise. And verily, a man continues to speak truthfully until he is written with God as a supremely veracious one (ṣiddīq). And verily, falsehood leads to wickedness (al-fujūr), and wickedness leads to the Fire. And verily, a man continues to lie until he is written with God as a liar (kadhdhāb).\" (1)", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 1226", "section_title_en": "The Book of Truthfulness (Ṣidq): Its Virtue and Reality — Section on the Virtue of Truthfulness", "section_index": 1226, "anchors": "- Root: صدق (Ṣ-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the firmness and soundness of a thing; a spear termed \"ṣadq\" is straight and rigid; truthfulness is speech that is firm, sound, and free of crookedness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — truthfulness and falsehood originate in speech, whether past or future, promise or otherwise; they occur by primary intent only in informative statements (khabar), not in other speech categories. Al-Raghib further notes the derived degrees: ṣidq (truthfulness), ṣiddīq (the supremely veracious — one whose truthfulness has become his very character), and ṣādiq al-waʿd (truthful in promise).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (literal): \"صدقته الحديث\" — to affirm someone's report as true. Majaz (metaphorical): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — a man truthful in his charge (i.e., steadfast and unfaltering in battle), and \"فرس ذو مصدق في الجري\" — a horse with genuine exertion in running. Thus ṣidq extends metaphorically to any state of firmness, steadfastness, and correspondence between inner conviction and outward action.\n- Root: بر (B-R-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Birr: expansive goodness, piety, and obedience; the \"barr\" (land) is so named for its expanse. It encompasses all acts of righteousness and kindness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-birr is the breadth of obedience to God through sincere intention and righteous action; it is the comprehensive term for all that God loves and approves.\n- Root: فجر (F-J-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Fujūr: the breaking forth and bursting open — the wickedness that breaks through the barrier of piety; the \"fājir\" is one who bursts out of obedience into transgression.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fujūr is the violation of the covenant of obedience, the breaking open of the seal of God-consciousness; it is the opposite of birr.\n- Root: كذب (K-DH-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Kidhb: falsehood — informing of a thing contrary to its reality; the root also carries the sense of deficiency and weakness (as in \"kadhaba\" — to fall short).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood is the counterpart of ṣidq — it is reporting what does not correspond to reality, whether in past, present, or future tense.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The grammatical structure \"يكتب عند الله صديقا\" (he is written with God as a ṣiddīq) involves the prepositional phrase \"عند الله\" interposed between the verb and its predicate accusative, a construction permissible when the interposed element is semantically integral to the judgment, as here where divine decree is the ground of the designation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "56": [{"arabic": "واذكر في الكتاب إسمعيل إنه كان صادق الوعد وكان رسولا نبيا وقال تعالى {واذكر في الكتاب إدريس إنه كان صديقا نبيا} وقال ابن عباس أربع من كن فيه فقد ربح الصدق والحياء وحسن الخلق والشكر وقال بشر ابن الحارث من عامل الله بالصدق استوحش من الناس وقال أبو عبد الله الرملي رأيت منصور الدينوري في المنام فقلت له ما فعل الله بك قال غفر لي ورحمني وأعطاني ما لم أؤمل فقلت له أحسن ما توجه العبد به إلى الله ماذا قال الصدق وأقبح ما توجه به الكذب وقال أبو سليمان اجعل الصدق مطيتك والحق سيفك والله تعالى غاية طلبتك وقال رجل لحكيم ما رأيت صادقا فقال له لو ك", "english": "God Most High has said: {«رجال صدقوا ما عاهدوا الله عليه»} — \"Among the believers are men who have been true to the covenant they made with God\" (Al-Aḥzāb 33:23). And the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Verily, truthfulness leads to righteousness (al-birr), and righteousness leads to Paradise. And verily, a man continues to speak truthfully until he is written with God as a supremely veracious one (ṣiddīq). And verily, falsehood leads to wickedness (al-fujūr), and wickedness leads to the Fire. And verily, a man continues to lie until he is written with God as a liar (kadhdhāb).\" (1)", "book_title": "Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness", "section_title_ar": "Section 1226", "section_title_en": "The Book of Truthfulness (Ṣidq): Its Virtue and Reality — Section on the Virtue of Truthfulness", "section_index": 1226, "anchors": "- Root: صدق (Ṣ-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the firmness and soundness of a thing; a spear termed \"ṣadq\" is straight and rigid; truthfulness is speech that is firm, sound, and free of crookedness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — truthfulness and falsehood originate in speech, whether past or future, promise or otherwise; they occur by primary intent only in informative statements (khabar), not in other speech categories. Al-Raghib further notes the derived degrees: ṣidq (truthfulness), ṣiddīq (the supremely veracious — one whose truthfulness has become his very character), and ṣādiq al-waʿd (truthful in promise).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (literal): \"صدقته الحديث\" — to affirm someone's report as true. Majaz (metaphorical): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — a man truthful in his charge (i.e., steadfast and unfaltering in battle), and \"فرس ذو مصدق في الجري\" — a horse with genuine exertion in running. Thus ṣidq extends metaphorically to any state of firmness, steadfastness, and correspondence between inner conviction and outward action.\n- Root: بر (B-R-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Birr: expansive goodness, piety, and obedience; the \"barr\" (land) is so named for its expanse. It encompasses all acts of righteousness and kindness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-birr is the breadth of obedience to God through sincere intention and righteous action; it is the comprehensive term for all that God loves and approves.\n- Root: فجر (F-J-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Fujūr: the breaking forth and bursting open — the wickedness that breaks through the barrier of piety; the \"fājir\" is one who bursts out of obedience into transgression.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fujūr is the violation of the covenant of obedience, the breaking open of the seal of God-consciousness; it is the opposite of birr.\n- Root: كذب (K-DH-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Kidhb: falsehood — informing of a thing contrary to its reality; the root also carries the sense of deficiency and weakness (as in \"kadhaba\" — to fall short).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood is the counterpart of ṣidq — it is reporting what does not correspond to reality, whether in past, present, or future tense.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The grammatical structure \"يكتب عند الله صديقا\" (he is written with God as a ṣiddīq) involves the prepositional phrase \"عند الله\" interposed between the verb and its predicate accusative, a construction permissible when the interposed element is semantically integral to the judgment, as here where divine decree is the ground of the designation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "59": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى فخلف من بعدهم أضاعوا الصلاة واتبعوا الشهوات فسوف يلقون غيا وقال تعالى فخلف من بعدهم خلف ورثوا الكتاب يأخذون عرض هذا الأدنى ويقولون سيغفر لنا وذم الله تعالى صاحب البستان إذا دخل جنته وقال ما أظن تبيده هذه أبدا وما أظن الساعة قائمة ولئن رددت إلى ربدى لأجدن خيرا منها منقلبا فإذا العبد المجتهد في الطاعات المجتنب للمعاصي حقيق بأن ينتظر من فضل الله تمام النعمة وما تمام النعمة إلا بدخول الجنة وأما العاصي فإذا تاب وتدارك جميع ما فرط منه من تقصير فحقيق بأن يرجو قبول التوبة وأما قبل التوبة إذا كان كارها للمعصية تس", "english": "It is fitting, therefore, that the servant's hope for forgiveness be measured against the hope of the cultivator. For whoever seeks good earth and casts into it good seed — neither rotten nor worm-eaten — then supplies it with what it needs, namely channeling water to it at its proper times, then clears away the thorns and weeds from the earth and everything that prevents the growth of the seed or corrupts it, then sits waiting for the bounty of God — may He be exalted — to repel the lightning-bolts and corrupting afflictions until the crop is completed and reaches its full extent — his waiting is named \"hope\" (rajā').", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 1029", "section_title_en": "The True Nature of Hope (Rajā') — Distinguishing Hope from Delusion and Wishful Thinking", "section_index": 1029, "anchors": "- Root: بذر (B-Dh-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Scattering and casting seed into the earth; the core physical act of sowing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التبذير: التفريق\" — dispersion; metaphorically applied to the squanderer of wealth. Here, Ghazali employs the literal sense (Haqiqah) of sowing seed as the foundational analogy for the cultivation of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"بذر الحب في الأرض\" (sowing grain in the earth). Metaphorical: \"بذر الله الخلق في الأرض\" (God scattered creation) and \"بذرت الأرض: أخرجت نباتها\" (the earth brought forth its plants). Ghazali uses the literal agricultural sense to ground the metaphysical analogy.\n- Root: سبب (S-B-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A rope, cord, or means by which one ascends or reaches something.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"سمي كل ما يتوصل به إلى شيء سببا\" — everything by which one arrives at a thing is called a cause/means. This is the technical sense operative here: the causes (asbāb) of hope are the means within the servant's capacity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a cord. Metaphorical: \"خيل مسببة\" (well-trained horses) and \"أشار إليه بالسبابة\" (pointing). Ghazali uses the term in its rational, causal sense — the means leading to an end.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transforming a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning of a thing from one aspect to another. The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and transformation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان\" (the teacher dismissed the boys) and \"رجل قلب حول\" (a man who turns matters over and manages them). The heart is the seat of this turning — the spiritual organ that must be cultivated or left barren.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication with 'innamā')\n  * The particle 'innamā' restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone. Ghazali employs this structure repeatedly to delimit the precise application of terms like 'rajā'' (hope), 'ghurūr' (delusion), and 'tamannī' (wishful thinking), ensuring each name is applied only to its exact referent.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "تكون ممن قال الله تعالى فيهم {فخلف من بعدهم خلف أضاعوا الصلاة} الآية وإنك تعامل من لا يجهل ويحفظ عليك من لا يغفل فداو دينك فقد دخله سقم وهيء رادك فقد حضر سفر بعيد {وما يخفى على الله من شيء في الأرض ولا في السماء} والسلام فهذه الأخبار والآثار تدل على ما في مخالطة السلاطين من الفتن وأنواع الفساد ولكن نفصل ذلك تفضيلا فقيها تميز فيه المحظور عن المكروه والمباح فنقول الداخل على السلطان متعرض لأن يعصي الله تعالى إما بفعله أو بسكوته وإما بقوله وإما باعتقاده فلا ينفك عن أحد هذه الأمور أما الفعل فالدخول عليهم في", "english": "I say: Know — may Allah guide you — that the scholar who draws near to a tyrannical ruler has betrayed his Book and his knowledge of the Sunnah of his Prophet Muhammad, peace and blessings be upon him. It is not thus that Allah took the covenant upon the scholars. Allah the Exalted said: {«لَتُبَيِّنُنَّهُ لِلنَّاسِ وَلَا تَكْتُمُونَهُ»} — \"You shall surely make it clear to the people and not conceal it\" (Āl ʿImrān 3:187).", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 390", "section_title_en": "Section 390 — The Severe Condemnation of Associating with Tyrannical Rulers and the Juridical Distinction of Its Prohibitions", "section_index": 390, "anchors": "- Root: ظلم (ẓ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is darkness (ẓulmah) — the absence of light; to place something in other than its proper position.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓulm is expressed as darkness (ẓulumāt) contrasted with light (nūr); it signifies ignorance, polytheism, and transgression. The wrongdoer (ẓālim) veils truth and acts in spiritual darkness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"الظلم ظلمات يوم القيامة\" — injustice is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection; justice is light. Metaphorical (majāz): \"أرض مظلومة\" — land never dug, untouched; the tyrant's soul is one upon which no ray of justice has fallen.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To possess, to own, to have dominion; al-milk is that which is under one's control.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Mulk is sovereignty over the affairs of rational beings through command and prohibition. True ownership (milk) entails the right of exclusive disposal.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: possession of property and dominion. Metaphorical: \"ملك نفسه عند الغضب\" — mastery over oneself in anger; the tyrant's seizure of property (ghaṣb) is an illegitimate extension of ownership.\n- Root: جمع (j-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To gather, to bring together parts into a whole.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Jam' is the bringing together of things by proximity; collective action compounds individual acts into a unified deed.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: assembling a crowd. Metaphorical: \"أمر بني فلان بجمع\" — a concealed matter, kept secret; the collective nature of transgression — each individual step contributing to a whole that constitutes usurpation.\n- Root: وجب (w-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be incumbent, to be due, to be necessary; to fall down dead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Wujūb is fixedness and establishment; that which is obligatory (wājib) — its neglect incurs blame, whether by reason or by sacred law.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجب البيع\" — the sale became binding. Metaphorical: \"ضربه فوجب\" — struck him so he fell dead; the obligatory nature of retaliation (qiṣāṣ) when collective acts cause destruction.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — The conditional clauses (fa-in kāna... fa-in kāna...) structure a graduated juridical analysis, each hypothesis (taqdīr) isolating a distinct legal category (ḥarām, makrūh, mubāḥ) with precise syntactic partitioning.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "62": [{"arabic": "لهم رزقهم فيها بكرة وعشيا أما إنه ليس ليل يكر الغدو على الرواح والرواح على الغدو وإن آخر من يدخل الجنة وأدناهم منزلة ليمد له في بصره وملكه مسيرة مائة عام في قصور من الذهب والفضة وخيام اللؤلؤ ويفسح له في بصره حتى ينظر إلى أقصاه كما ينظر إلى أدناه يغدى عليهم بسبعين ألف صحفة من ذهب ويراح عليهم بمثلها في كل صحفة لون ليس في الأخرى مثله ويجد طعم آخره كما يجد طعم أوله وإن في الجنة لياقوتة فيها سبعون ألف دار في كل دار سبعون ألف بيت ليس فيها صدع ولا ثقب وقال مجاهد إن أدنى أهل الجن", "english": "And Mujāhid said: \"The lowest of the people of Paradise in station is one who travels through his dominion for a thousand years, seeing its farthest as he sees its nearest. And the highest of them is the one who gazes upon his Lord morning and evening.\" And Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib said: \"There is none among the people of Paradise except that in his hand are three bracelets: a bracelet of gold, a bracelet of pearl, and a bracelet of silver.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1358", "section_title_en": "The Vision of Allah and the Gaze Upon His Face — The Supreme Bliss of the Hereafter", "section_index": 1358, "anchors": "- Root: حسن (ḥ-s-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of beauty, goodness, and excellence in form and act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسنى\" denotes the superlative good (al-ḥusnā), and \"زيادة\" (increase) is the added bounty beyond merit — a theological term for the beatific vision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for moral beauty; metaphorical (majāz) elevation when applied to divine reward, where \"increase\" transcends all corporeal delights.\n- Root: رءي (r-'-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Visual perception (ru'yā) and sight (naẓar), both sensory and intellectual.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, \"ru'yat Allāh\" is the vision of the divine essence — an immaterial perception granted to the blessed, not a corporeal sight of a spatial object.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical sight; metaphorical transfer (naql) when applied to God, signifying direct, unveiled apprehension of His glory.\n- Root: نعم (n-'-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النعمة\" is the state of well-being; \"النعمى\" is the superlative felicity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة\" as the good state (ḥālah ḥasanah), and \"النعمى\" as the ultimate bliss — here contrasted with the lesser delights of Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for sensory comfort; metaphorical for the spiritual joy of divine proximity, which surpasses all bodily pleasures.\n- Root: اهل ('-h-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: People of a household or place; \"أهل الجنة\" — the inhabitants of Paradise.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أهل الرجل\" — those united by kinship, religion, or abode; here, the denizens of the Garden as a community of the blessed.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for residents; metaphorical extension to those who merit a station by virtue of their deeds.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used in the closing exhortation to restrict the servant's aspiration solely to the vision of the Lord, excluding all lesser aims.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "68": [{"arabic": "العرض على الله تعالى تصديقا لقوله تعالى {فوربك لنحشرنهم والشياطين ثم لنحضرنهم حول جهنم جثيا} فتفكر فى حالك وحال قلبك هنالك", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted said: \"The son of Adam has reviled Me, and he had no right to revile Me; and he has denied Me, and he had no right to deny Me. As for his reviling of Me, it is his saying that I have a son. And as for his denial of Me, it is his saying: 'He will never bring me back as He created me at first.'\" {«شتمني ابن آدم وما ينبغي له أن يشتمني وكذبني وما ينبغي له أن يكذبني...»}", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1335", "section_title_en": "On the Terrors of the Trumpet-Blast and the Awakening for the Resurrection", "section_index": 1335, "anchors": "- Root: نفخ (N-F-Kh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To blow into a thing, to inflate; the breath of wind entering a vessel or body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The divine act of breathing spirit into creation {«ونفخت فيه من روحي»}; here, the eschatological blast that annihilates and then revives all beings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage of blowing into the Trumpet (الصور); metaphorical extension to the \"blast of spring\" reviving the earth—here applied to the two cosmic blasts of death and resurrection.\n- Root: صور (S-W-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Form, image, shape; also a horn or trumpet (the instrument of the blast).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The perceptible form of bodies and the intelligible form of souls; here, the Trumpet (الصور) as the instrument of divine command that re-forms and re-shapes creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the horn (القرن) held by Israfil; metaphorical: the \"inclination\" (صورة) of hearts toward terror and anticipation.\n- Root: موت (M-W-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of life; the state of the corpse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as the separation of the soul from the body; the \"first death\" of the blast and the \"second death\" of the wicked.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; metaphorical: the \"death\" of the heart through heedlessness, and the reviving of dead lands by rain—here, the reviving of bodies by the second blast.\n- Root: قبر (Q-B-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The grave as the abode of the dead; burial.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The grave as the intermediate state (barzakh) before resurrection; {«حتى زرتم المقابر»} as a metaphor for death.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the grave and its inhabitants; metaphorical: the \"raising of the nose\" (قبراه) of the proud—contrasted here with the humility of the dead rising from their graves.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize that true faith is not mere verbal assent but heartfelt conviction, as in \"إنما فتور البواطن... لقلة الفهم\" (The languor of the inner selves is only due to the lack of understanding).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "69": [{"arabic": "عتيا على الله تعالى فقال ثم لننزعن من كل شيعة أيهم أشد على الرحمن عتيا وقال تعالى فالذين لا يؤمنون بالآخرة قلوبهم منكرة وهم مستكبرون وقال عز وجل يقول الذين استضعفوا للذين استكبروا لولا أنتم لكنا مؤمنين وقال تعالى إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي سيدخلون جهنم داخرين وقال تعالى سأصرف عن آياتي الذين يتكبرون في الأرض بغير الحق قيل في التفسير سأرفع فهم القرآن عن قلوبهم وفي بعض التفاسير سأحجب قلوبهم عن الملكوت وقال ابن جريج سأصرفهم عن أن يتفكروا فيها ويعتبروا بها ولذلك قال المسيح عليه السلام إن الزرع ينبت في السهل ولا ينبت على الص", "english": "When this [pride] appears upon the bodily limbs, it is called \"behaving arrogantly\" (takabbur); and when it does not appear [outwardly], it is said that in his soul there is pride (kibr). The root [of the matter] is the moral trait (khuluq) that resides in the soul — namely, the soul's taking comfort and repose in seeing itself as above the one over whom it acts proudly. For pride necessarily requires an object over whom one is proud and a quality by virtue of which one is proud; and by this [requirement], pride is distinguished from self-conceit ('ujb), as will come later. For self-conceit does not require an object other than the self-conceited one. Indeed, were a human being created alone, it is conceivable that he would be self-conceited, but it is not conceivable that he would be proud except when there is another alongside him, and he sees himself as above that other in the attributes of perfection. Only at that point does he become proud. Nor is it sufficient that he deem himself great in order to be proud — for a man may deem himself great yet see another as greater than himself or equal to himself, and so he does not act proudly toward him. Nor is it sufficient that he hold another in contempt — for even so, if he saw himself as more contemptible [than that other], he would not be proud; and if he saw the other as his equal, he would not be proud. Rather, he must assign to himself a rank and to the other a rank, then see his own rank as above the other's rank. Upon these three beliefs, the trait of pride comes to be in him. It is not that this perception alone negates pride; rather, this perception and this conviction blow into him [a wind of vanity], and there arises in his heart self-complacency, a thrill, joy, and repose in what he has come to believe, and a sense of vainglory in himself on account of that. That vainglory, that thrill, and that repose in the belief — that is the trait of pride. For this reason, the Prophet — God's peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"I seek refuge in You from the puff of pride (nafkhat al-kibriyā').\" And the hadith has preceded: \"None shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a mustard seed of pride.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 854", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Pride (Kibr) and Its Manifestation in the Soul and Limbs", "section_index": 854, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (K-B-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of bigness, greatness, and seniority in age or rank; al-Farahidi notes its use for physical growth and maturity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — great and small are relative terms, applied by comparison; in Kalam, pride (kibr) is a state of the soul that esteems itself above another in perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"كبر الأمر، وخطب كبير\" — to be great in magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"كبر عليه\" — to find something burdensome or grievous; the moral-spiritual sense of kibr is a metaphorical extension of physical magnitude to the soul's self-assessment.\n- Root: نفس (N-F-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates and is the seat of inner states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul; also the very essence (al-dhat), as in \"ويحذركم الله نفسه\" {آل عمران/ 30}, where His \"self\" is His essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس\" — precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — \"his self\" for \"his blood\"; here, the soul's inner disposition (khuluq) is the locus where pride takes root before it appears on the limbs.\n- Root: وفي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, and reaching full measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى بعهده\" — to fulfill one's covenant fully.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham. Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة\" — to exceed a hundred; in this text, the \"full measure of a mustard seed\" (مثقال ذرة) of pride is the complete, realized quantity that bars one from Paradise.\n- Root: خلق (Kh-L-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, shaping, and fashioning; also innate disposition and moral character.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — its root is right measure and estimation; used for God's origination of things and for the moral constitution of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خلق الخراز الأديم\" — the leatherworker measures the hide before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق\" — God originated creation upon a measure dictated by wisdom; hence khuluq is the soul's fixed moral form.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — used to delimit the precise definition of kibr as a specific soul-state distinct from 'ujb (self-conceit), and to restrict the cause of exclusion from Paradise to the presence of pride in the heart.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "71": [{"arabic": "وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا ونذر الظالمين فيها جثيا ولذلك قال الخائفون من السلف إنما خوفنا لأنا تيقنا أنا على النار واردون وشككنا في النجاة ولما روى الحسن الخبر الوارد فيمن يخرج من النار بعد ألف عام وأنه ينادي يا حنان يا منان (1) قال الحسن يا ليتني كنت ذلك الرجل واعلم أن في الأخبار ما يدل على أن آخر من يخرج من النار بعد سبعة آلاف سنة وأن الاختلاف في المدة بين اللحظة وبين سبعة آلاف سنة حتى قد يجوز بعضهم على النار كبرق خاطف ولا يكون له فيها لبث وبين اللحظة وبين سبعة آلاف سنة درجات متفاوتة من اليوم والأسب", "english": "The fire of Hell is the coldest of fires [in comparison to the fire of separation]. And you should not deny this in the world of the Hereafter, for it has a witnessed parallel in the world of this world. Indeed, one has been seen who was overcome by ecstasy (wajd) and walked upon fire and upon the roots of cane, his feet being cut, yet he did not feel it, due to the extreme dominance of what was in his heart. And you see the angry man, when anger overcomes him in battle, receiving wounds and not feeling them at that moment, because anger is a fire in the heart. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: {«الغضب قطعة من النار»} \"Anger is a piece of fire.\" The burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) is more severe than the burning of bodies, and the more severe annuls the sensation of the weaker, as you observe. For destruction from fire and the sword is only inasmuch as they separate between two parts that are linked one to the other by the bond of composition (ta'līf) possible in bodies. So that which separates between the heart and its Beloved, to which it is linked by a bond of composition far more firm than the composition of bodies, is more intensely painful—if you are among the people of insight (abṣār) and the people of hearts. And it is not far-fetched that he who has no heart should not perceive the severity of this pain and should deem it trivial in comparison to bodily pain. For a boy, if he were given the choice between the pain of being deprived of a ball and a polo-stick, and the pain of being deprived of the rank of a Sultan, he would not feel the pain of deprivation from the Sultan's rank at all, nor would he count that as a pain. And the enemy in the field with the polo-stick is more beloved to him than a thousand thrones of the Sultan with sitting upon them. Nay, even one overcome by the lust of the belly, if given the choice between porridge (harīsah) and sweets (ḥalwā) and between a beautiful deed by which he conquers enemies and delights friends, he would prefer the porridge and sweets. All of this is due to the absence of the meaning (maʿnā) by whose existence rank becomes beloved, and the presence of the meaning by whose existence food becomes delicious. This is for one whom the attributes of beasts and predators have enslaved, and in whom the attributes of the angels have not appeared—those attributes which are not suited by, nor delight in, anything but proximity to the Lord of the Worlds, and which are pained only by distance and the veil (ḥijāb). And just as taste is only in the tongue and hearing only in the ears, so this attribute is only in the heart. So he who has no heart has no such sense, like he who has no hearing or sight has no pleasure in melodies or the beauty of forms and colors. And not every human has a heart; were it so, the saying of God, Most High, would not be sound: {«إن في ذلك لذكرى لمن كان له قلب»} \"Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart\" (Q. 50:37). Thus He made the one who does not heed the Quran devoid of a heart. And by \"heart\" I do not mean this [organ] which the ribs of the chest encompass. Rather, I mean the secret (sirr) which is of the world of the Command (ʿālam al-amr). The flesh which is of the world of Creation (ʿālam al-khalq) is its Throne (ʿarsh), the chest is its Footstool (kursī), and the rest of the limbs are its domain and kingdom. And to God belongs the Creation and the Command altogether. But that secret, concerning which God, Most High, said: {«قل الروح من أمر ربي»} \"Say: The Spirit is of the Command of my Lord\" (Q. 17:85), is the commander and the king, because between the world of the Command and the world of Creation there is an ordering, and the world of the Command is commander over the world of Creation. It is the subtle divine reality (al-laṭīfah) which, when it is sound, the rest of the body is sound for its sake. Whoever knows it has known his own self, and whoever knows his own self has known his Lord. At that point, the servant smells the beginnings of the fragrance of the meaning folded under his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: {«إن الله خلق آدم على صورته»} \"God created Adam upon His image.\" He looks with the eye of mercy upon those who adhere to its literal wording and upon those who are obstinate in the path of its interpretation (ta'wīl). And even though His mercy for those adhering to the letter is greater than His mercy for the obstinate interpreters, because mercy is proportional to the affliction, and the affliction of those [literalists] is greater—even if they share in the affliction of being deprived of the reality of the matter—for the Reality (al-ḥaqīqah) is the bounty of God which He gives to whom He wills, and God is the Possessor of Great Bounty. And His Wisdom is singled out for whom He wills: {«ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتي خيرا كثيرا»} \"And whoever is given wisdom has been given much good\" (Q. 2:269). Let us return to the purpose, for we have drawn out the length and lengthened the breath in a matter that is higher than the sciences of transactions (muʿāmalāt) which we intend in this book. It has become clear that the rank of destruction is only for the ignorant deniers. And the testimony of that from the Book of God and the Sunnah of His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him, is beyond enumeration, so we have not cited it.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 930", "section_title_en": "The Ranks of the Hereafter: On the Distribution of Degrees and the States of the Saved, the Punished, and the Victorious", "section_index": 930, "anchors": "- Root: ح ر ق (ḥ-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, and the fire's consumption of its fuel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The fire of the Hereafter (Nār) is a created reality, but its perception is tied to the spiritual state of the perceiver.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical burning; metaphorical (Majaz) for the burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) by overwhelming love or anger.\n- Root: ق ل ب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart (Qalb) is the seat of perception and understanding, distinct from the physical organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the physical heart; metaphorical for the spiritual subtlety (Laṭīfah) that is the locus of divine knowledge and love.\n- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know, to perceive a thing by its true nature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge (ʿIlm) is the apprehension of a thing's reality, which is the basis for distinguishing the ranks of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for acquired knowledge; metaphorical for the \"unveiling\" (Kashf) knowledge granted to the knowers (ʿĀrifūn).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — Used to distinguish the specific referents of terms like \"heart\" (Qalb) and \"faith\" (Īmān), ensuring the precise meaning is not conflated with common usage.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا أي الذين كان قربهم إلى الصراط المستقيم أكثر من بعدهم عنه ولأجل عسر الاستقامة وجب على كل عبد أن يدعو الله تعالى في كل يوم سبع عشرة مرة في قوله اهدنا الصراط المستقيم إذ وجب قراءة الفاتحة في كل ركعة فقد روي أن بعضهم رأى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في المنام فقال قد قلت يا رسول الله شيبتني هود فلم قلت ذلك فقال عليه السلام لقوله تعالى فاستقم كما أمرت فالاستقامة على سواء السبيل في غاية الغموض ولكن ينبغي أن يجتهد الإنسان في القرب من الاستقامة إن لم يقدر على", "english": "And for this reason, he is never free from a torment of some kind and a passage over the Fire, even if it be like the flash of lightning. God Almighty said: {«وَإِن مِّنكُمْ إِلَّا وَارِدُهَا ۚ كَانَ عَلَىٰ رَبِّكَ حَتْمًا مَّقْضِيًّا * ثُمَّ نُنَجِّي الَّذِينَ اتَّقَوا»} — \"And there is none of you but will pass over it (the Fire): this is with your Lord a decree which must be accomplished. Then We shall save those who guarded against evil\" (Q. 19:71-72) — that is, those whose nearness to the straight path was greater than their distance from it. And because of the difficulty of uprightness (al-istiqāmah), it became incumbent upon every servant to supplicate God Almighty seventeen times each day in His saying: {«اهْدِنَا الصِّرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ»} — \"Guide us to the straight path\" (Q. 1:6) — since the recitation of the Opening Chapter (al-Fātiḥah) is obligatory in every unit of prayer (rakʿah).", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 625", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 625: The Straight Path, Its Difficulty, and the Means of Knowing One's Own Faults", "section_index": 625, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To arrive at water, to come to a place of watering; to approach, to present oneself\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic context of {«وَإِن مِّنكُمْ إِلَّا وَارِدُهَا»} (Q. 19:71), denotes the universal passage over the Fire — a necessary approach, not necessarily abiding therein\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) sense of arriving at water; here metaphorically (majaz) extended to the obligatory crossing over the Fire on the Day of Resurrection\n- Root: قرب (qaruba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank; proximity\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nearness to God is by virtue and knowledge, not by physical distance; al-qurb (nearness) is the opposite of al-buʿd (distance)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal spatial proximity; metaphorical proximity in degree of obedience and alignment with the straight path\n- Root: عيب (ʿayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A defect, blemish, imperfection; also curdled milk (al-ʿāʾib)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Moral deficiency in character or action; that which renders one blameworthy\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a blemish in a garment or vessel; metaphorical (majaz): \"He is the repository (ʿaybah) of so-and-so's secrets\" — i.e., the one entrusted with what is hidden; here, the inward faults of the soul\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; breath; essence\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the locus of both blameworthy and praiseworthy traits; in {«وَنَفْسٍ وَمَا سَوَّاهَا»} (Q. 91:7), that which is inspired with its wickedness and its righteousness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self; metaphorical: \"he poured out his nafs\" meaning his blood; here, the inner self with its hidden defects\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"فمن كانت بصيرته نافذة لم تخف عليه عيوبه\" employs conditional restriction (al-sharṭ) to delimit the scope of insight; \"إنما نكايتها على البدن\" uses innamā for exclusive predication, restricting the harm of the scorpion's sting to the body alone", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "لأن الأخبار مصرحة بأن العصاة يعذبون (1) بل قوله تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كالصريح في أن ذلك لا بد منه للكل إذ لا يخلو مؤمن عن ذنب يرتكبه وقوله تعالى لا يصلاها إلا الأشقى الذي كذب وتولى أراد به من جماعة مخصوصين أو أراد بالأشقى شخصا معينا أيضا وقوله تعالى كلما ألقى فيها فوج سألهم خزنتها أي فوج من الكفار وتخصيص العمومات قريب ومن هذه الآية وقع للأشعري وطائفة من المتكلمين إنكار صيغ العموم وأن هذه الألفاظ يتوقف فيها إلى ظهور قرينة تدل على معناها وأما المعتزلة فشبهتهم قوله تعالى وإني لغفار لمن تاب وآمن وعمل صالحا ثم اهت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "الصالحات وقوله تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا {ثم قال} ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا وقوله تعالى ومن يعص الله ورسوله فإن له نار جهنم وكل آية ذكر الله عز وجل العمل الصالح فيها مقرونا بالإيمان وقوله تعالى ومن يقتل مؤمنا متعمدا فجزاؤه جهنم خالدا فيها وهذه العمومات أيضا مخصوصة بدليل قوله تعالى ويغفر ما دون ذلك لمن يشاء فينبغي أن تبقى له مشيئة في مغفرة ما سوى الشرك وكذلك قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم يخرج من النار من كان في قلبه مثقال ذرة من إيمان وقوله تعالى إنا لا نضيع أجر من أحسن عملا وقوله تعا", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "72": [{"arabic": "الصالحات وقوله تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا {ثم قال} ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا وقوله تعالى ومن يعص الله ورسوله فإن له نار جهنم وكل آية ذكر الله عز وجل العمل الصالح فيها مقرونا بالإيمان وقوله تعالى ومن يقتل مؤمنا متعمدا فجزاؤه جهنم خالدا فيها وهذه العمومات أيضا مخصوصة بدليل قوله تعالى ويغفر ما دون ذلك لمن يشاء فينبغي أن تبقى له مشيئة في مغفرة ما سوى الشرك وكذلك قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم يخرج من النار من كان في قلبه مثقال ذرة من إيمان وقوله تعالى إنا لا نضيع أجر من أحسن عملا وقوله تعالى إن الله لا يضيع أجر المح", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "77": [{"arabic": "فأنزل الله تعالى قوله أفرأيت الذي كفر بآياتنا وقال لأوتين مالا وولدا (1) حديث إن الله يحمي عبده من الدنيا وهو يحبه الحديث أخرجه الترمذي وحسنه والحاكم وصححه من حديث قتادة بن النعمان هكذا ورد في الخبر عن سيد البشر وكان أرباب البصائر إذا أقبلت عليهم الدنيا حزنوا وقالوا ذنب عجلت عقوبته ورأوا ذلك علامة المقت والإهمال وإذا أقبل عليهم الفقر قالوا مرحبا بشعار الصالحين والمغرور إذا أقبلت عليه الدنيا ظن أنها كرامة من الله وإذا صرفت عنه ظن أنها هوان كما أخبر الله تعالى عنه إذ قال فأما الإنسان إذا ما ابتلاه ربه فأكرمه ون", "english": "It was said of that sort, so they are falsehoods (akādhīb). And even if it were so, then \"there will surely be for me in Paradise better than this\" {«لَيَكُونَنَّ لِي فِي الْجَنَّةِ خَيْرٌ مِنْ هَذَا»}. Likewise, God Most High described the saying of al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil when he said, \"I will surely be given wealth and children\" {«لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا»}. So God Most High replied to him: \"Has he looked into the unseen, or has he taken with the Most Merciful a covenant? No indeed (kallā)\" {«أَطَّلَعَ الْغَيْبَ أَمِ اتَّخَذَ عِنْدَ الرَّحْمَنِ عَهْدًا كَلَّا»}. And it is related from Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt that he said: I had a debt owed to me by al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil, so I came demanding payment, but he did not pay me. So I said, \"I will indeed take it from you in the Hereafter.\" He said to me, \"When you come to the Hereafter, I will have there wealth and children, and I will pay you from it.\" So God Most High revealed His saying: \"Have you seen the one who disbelieved in Our signs and said, 'I will surely be given wealth and children'?\" {«أَفَرَأَيْتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ بِآيَاتِنَا وَقَالَ لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا»}. (1) The hadith \"God protects His servant from the world while loving him\" — narrated by al-Tirmidhī who deemed it good (ḥasan), and by al-Ḥākim who deemed it authentic (ṣaḥīḥ), from the report of Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān. Thus it came in the report from the master of mankind (peace be upon him).", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 887", "section_title_en": "The Delusion of Wealth and the Reality of Divine Trial — Section 887", "section_index": 887, "anchors": "- Root: كلا (Kalla)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: An emphatic particle of denial and refutation; signifies categorical rejection of a prior claim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used to negate and refute utterly; here, it rebuts the deluded assumption that wealth signals divine favor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — a decisive \"No!\"; Metaphorical (Majaz) — not merely denying words but the very presumption underlying them.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥadatha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To originate, to be new; in Kalam, that which comes into being after non-existence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination of a thing after it was not; divine acts of trial are originated events.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — new occurrences; Metaphorical — \"محدث\" as one whose inner state is renewed by divine testing.\n- Root: كذب (Kadhaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To lie, to falsify; a false report or claim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood in word and deed; here, the disbeliever's boast is exposed as self-deception.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — verbal lying; Metaphorical — \"كذبهما جميعا\" (he declared both of them liars) — attributing falsity to the two human assumptions about wealth and poverty.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما هو ابتلاء\" restricts the reality of wealth and poverty solely to divine testing, excluding the deluded interpretations of honor or humiliation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "78": [{"arabic": "الله تعالى ردا عليه أطلع الغيب أم اتخذ عند الرحمن عهدا كلا وروي عن خباب بن الأرت أنه قال كان لي على العاص بن وائل دين فجئت أتقاضاه فلم يقض لي فقلت إني آخذه في الآخرة فقال لي إذا صرت إلى الآخرة فإن لي هناك مالا وولدا أقضيك منه فأنزل الله تعالى قوله أفرأيت الذي كفر بآياتنا وقال لأوتين مالا وولدا (1) حديث إن الله يحمي عبده من الدنيا وهو يحبه الحديث أخرجه الترمذي وحسنه والحاكم وصححه من حديث قتادة بن النعمان هكذا ورد في الخبر عن سيد البشر وكان أرباب البصائر إذا أقبلت عليهم الدنيا حزنوا وقالوا ذنب عجلت عقوبته", "english": "It was said of that sort, so they are falsehoods (akādhīb). And even if it were so, then \"there will surely be for me in Paradise better than this\" {«لَيَكُونَنَّ لِي فِي الْجَنَّةِ خَيْرٌ مِنْ هَذَا»}. Likewise, God Most High described the saying of al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil when he said, \"I will surely be given wealth and children\" {«لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا»}. So God Most High replied to him: \"Has he looked into the unseen, or has he taken with the Most Merciful a covenant? No indeed (kallā)\" {«أَطَّلَعَ الْغَيْبَ أَمِ اتَّخَذَ عِنْدَ الرَّحْمَنِ عَهْدًا كَلَّا»}. And it is related from Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt that he said: I had a debt owed to me by al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil, so I came demanding payment, but he did not pay me. So I said, \"I will indeed take it from you in the Hereafter.\" He said to me, \"When you come to the Hereafter, I will have there wealth and children, and I will pay you from it.\" So God Most High revealed His saying: \"Have you seen the one who disbelieved in Our signs and said, 'I will surely be given wealth and children'?\" {«أَفَرَأَيْتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ بِآيَاتِنَا وَقَالَ لَأُوتَيَنَّ مَالًا وَوَلَدًا»}. (1) The hadith \"God protects His servant from the world while loving him\" — narrated by al-Tirmidhī who deemed it good (ḥasan), and by al-Ḥākim who deemed it authentic (ṣaḥīḥ), from the report of Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān. Thus it came in the report from the master of mankind (peace be upon him).", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 887", "section_title_en": "The Delusion of Wealth and the Reality of Divine Trial — Section 887", "section_index": 887, "anchors": "- Root: كلا (Kalla)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: An emphatic particle of denial and refutation; signifies categorical rejection of a prior claim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used to negate and refute utterly; here, it rebuts the deluded assumption that wealth signals divine favor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — a decisive \"No!\"; Metaphorical (Majaz) — not merely denying words but the very presumption underlying them.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥadatha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To originate, to be new; in Kalam, that which comes into being after non-existence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination of a thing after it was not; divine acts of trial are originated events.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — new occurrences; Metaphorical — \"محدث\" as one whose inner state is renewed by divine testing.\n- Root: كذب (Kadhaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To lie, to falsify; a false report or claim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood in word and deed; here, the disbeliever's boast is exposed as self-deception.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — verbal lying; Metaphorical — \"كذبهما جميعا\" (he declared both of them liars) — attributing falsity to the two human assumptions about wealth and poverty.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما هو ابتلاء\" restricts the reality of wealth and poverty solely to divine testing, excluding the deluded interpretations of honor or humiliation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "84": [{"arabic": "إلى نفسه وبكى على عدد ذنوبه ثم قرأ هذه الآية إنما نعد لهم عدا يعني الأنفاس آخر العدد خروج نفسك آخر العدد فراق أهلك آخر العدد دخولك في قبرك واجتهد أبو موسى الأشعري قبل موته اجتهادا شديدا فقيل له لو أمسكت أو رفقت بنفسك بعض الرفق فقال إن الخيل إذا أرسلت فقاربت رأس مجراها أخرجت جميع ما عندها والذي بقي من أجلى أقل من ذلك قال فلم يزل على ذلك حتى مات وكان يقول لامرأته شدي رحلك فليس على جهنم معبرة وقال بعض الخلفاء على منبره عباد الله اتقوا الله ما استطعتم وكونوا قوما صيح بهم فانتبهوا وعلموا أن الدنيا ليست", "english": "I say: The hadith was transmitted by al-Tirmidhi and deemed sound (ḥasan) from the narration of Ubayy ibn Kaʿb (5): a hadith that when he (the Prophet, peace be upon him) sensed from his companions heedlessness or negligence, he would call out to them in a raised voice: \"Death has come upon you.\" This hadith was transmitted by Ibn Abi al-Dunya in *Qaṣr al-Amal* from the narration of Zayd al-Sulaymi as a mursal report (6). The hadith of Abu Hurayrah: \"I am the warner, death is the transformer, and the Hour is the appointed meeting\" — transmitted by Ibn Abi al-Dunya in *Qaṣr al-Amal* and by Abu al-Qasim al-Baghawi with an isnad containing some weakness (7). The hadith of Ibn ʿUmar: \"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, went out while the sun was at the tips of the palm fronds and said: 'What remains of this world is but like what remains of this day of ours, relative to what has passed of it'\" — transmitted by Ibn Abi al-Dunya therein with a sound isnad, and al-Tirmidhi transmitted something similar from the narration of Abu Saʿid and deemed it sound (8). The hadith: \"The likeness of this world is like a garment torn from its beginning to its end\" — transmitted by Ibn Abi al-Dunya therein from the narration of Anas, but it is not authentic (9). Ibn Masʿud, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, recited: {فمن يرد الله أن يهديه يشرح صدره للإسلام} \"Whomsoever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast to Islam\" {فقال} \"Then he said: 'When the light enters the breast, it expands.' It was said: 'O Messenger of God, is there a sign of this that is recognized?' PageV04P459 {قال} He said: 'Yes: withdrawal from the abode of delusion, turning in repentance to the abode of permanence, and preparation for death before its descent.'\" Al-Suddi said concerning His saying: {الذي خلق الموت والحياة ليبلوكم أيكم أحسن عملا} \"He who created death and life to test you, which of you is best in deed\" — meaning: which of you is most mindful of death, best prepared for it, and most fearful and cautious of it. Hudhayfah said: \"There is no morning or evening except that a caller calls out: 'O people, the departure! The departure!'\" The confirmation of that is His saying, the Exalted: {إنها لإحدى الكبر * نذيرا للبشر * لمن شاء منكم أن يتقدم أو يتأخر} \"Indeed, it is one of the greatest calamities, a warning to mankind, for whoever among you wishes to advance or delay\" — regarding death. Suhaym, the freed slave of Banu Tamim, said: I sat with ʿAmir ibn ʿAbd Allah while he was praying, and he shortened his prayer, then turned to me and said: \"Relieve me of your need, for I am hastening.\" I said: \"What are you hastening for?\" He said: \"The angel of death, may God have mercy on you.\" He said: So I rose from him, and he returned to his prayer. Dawud al-Ta'i passed by, and a man asked him about a hadith, so he said: \"Leave me, for I am only hastening against the departure of my soul.\" ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, said: \"Deliberation is good in everything except in deeds of goodness for the Hereafter.\" Al-Mundhir said: I heard Malik ibn Dinar saying to himself: \"Woe to you, hasten before the matter comes upon you. Woe to you, hasten before the matter comes upon you\" — repeating that sixty times, while I heard him and he did not see me. Al-Hasan used to say in his exhortation: \"Hasten, hasten! For it is but breaths — if they are held back, your deeds by which you draw near to God, the Mighty and Majestic, are cut off. May God have mercy on a man who looked to his own soul and wept over the number of his sins.\" Then he recited this verse: {إنما نعد لهم عدا} \"We only count for them a counting\" — meaning the breaths. The end of the counting is the departure of your soul; the end of the counting is the parting from your family; the end of the counting is your entry into your grave. Abu Musa al-Ashʿari strove with intense exertion before his death, and it was said to him: \"Would you not hold back, or show yourself some gentleness?\" He said: \"When horses are released and approach the end of their course, they bring out all that they have. What remains of my term is less than that.\" He said: He continued in that state until he died. And he used to say to his wife: \"Tighten your saddle, for there is no crossing over Hellfire.\" One of the caliphs said from his pulpit: \"O servants of God, fear God as much as you are able, and be a people who were called out to, so they awoke, and knew that this world is not for them a permanent abode, so they exchanged it, and prepared for death, for it has overshadowed you. And depart, for the journey has become urgent for you. Indeed, a goal that the moment diminishes and the hour demolishes is worthy of a short duration. Indeed, an absent one whom the two new ones — night and day — hasten is deserving of a swift return. Indeed, an arrival that resolves in triumph or misery is worthy of the best provision. So let him meet his Lord — whoever advises his own soul, presents his repentance, and overcomes his desire — for his term is concealed from him, his hope deceives him, and Satan is entrusted to him, promising him repentance to postpone it, and beautifying disobedience to him to commit it, until his death descends upon him when he is most heedless of it. Indeed, between each of you and Paradise or Hellfire is nothing but death, that it should alight upon him. So what a regret for one who is heedless, or whose life is a proof against him, and whose days lead him to misery. May God make us and you among those whom prosperity does not make insolent, whom disobedience does not hold back from obedience to God, and upon whom regret does not alight after death. Indeed, He is Hearing of supplication, and in His hand is goodness always, and He does what He wills.\" Some of the exegetes said concerning His saying, the Exalted: {فتنتم أنفسكم} \"You tried yourselves\" — meaning: with desires and pleasures; {وتربصتم} \"and you waited\" — meaning: for repentance; {وارتبتم} \"and you doubted\" — meaning: you were skeptical, until the command of God came — meaning: death; {وغركم بالله الغرور} \"and the deluder deluded you concerning God\" — meaning: Satan. Al-Hasan said: \"Be patient and be firm, for it is but a few days, and you are but a standing caravan — soon one of you will be called, and he will respond without turning back. So move on with the righteous deeds that are in your possession.\" Ibn Masʿud said: \"There is none of you who wakes up except that he is a guest, and what he has is a loan. The guest departs, and the loan is returned.\" Abu ʿUbaydah al-Baji said: We entered upon al-Hasan in his illness from which he died, and he said: \"Welcome to you, and greetings. May God greet you with peace, and settle us and you in the abode of permanence. This is a good outward sign if you are patient, truthful, and God-fearing. So let not your share of this report — may God have mercy on you — be merely that you hear it with this ear and let it out from this ear. For whoever has seen Muhammad, peace be upon him, has seen him going forth early.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1291", "section_title_en": "The Haste for Death and Readiness for the Hereafter — Section 1291", "section_index": 1291, "anchors": "- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes cessation of vital faculty; \"مات موتة\" as a singular event of death.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as transition from worldly life to the reckoning; linked to \"أجل\" (appointed term) and divine decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) = biological death; Metaphorical (Majaz) = \"إماتة\" of the heart via heedlessness, and \"الموت المغير\" as the transformative destroyer of states.\n- Root: بدر (b-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes hastening, rushing to a task; \"بادر\" implies competing with time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, \"المبادر\" is the soul's urgency toward obedience before the term expires.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = physical speed; Metaphorical = spiritual vigilance against procrastination (تسويف).\n- Root: عرض (ʿ-r-ḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes presenting, exposing; \"العارية\" (loan) as something transiently possessed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Worldly life as \"عارية\" (borrowed thing) from God, to be returned at death.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = loaned object; Metaphorical = the body and lifespan as a trust (أمانة) from the Creator.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التوكيد بالتكرار (Emphatic Repetition) — Repetition of phrases like \"ويحك بادري\" and \"المبادرة المبادرة\" serves as syntactic emphasis (توكيد لفظي) to intensify the exhortation, a structure Sibawayh treats as permissible for rhetorical force without redundancy.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "85": [{"arabic": "خثعم فقرأ عليه {يوم نحشر المتقين إلى الرحمن وفدا ونسوق المجرمين إلى جهنم وردا} فقال أنا من المجرمين ولست من المتقين أعد على القول أيها القارئ فأعادها عليه فشهق شهقة فلحق بالآخرة وقرئ عند يحيى البكاء {ولو ترى إذ وقفوا على ربهم} فصاح صيحة مكث منها مريضا أربعة أشهر يعاد من أطراف البصرة وقال مالك بن دينار بينما أنا أطوف بالبيت إذا أنا بجويرة متعبدة متعلقة بأستار الكعبة وهي تقول يا رب كم شهوة ذهبت لذاتها وبقيت تبعاتها يا رب أما كان لك أدب وعقوبة إلا النار وتبكى فما زال ذلك مقامها حتى طلع الفجر قال مالك فلما رأيت ذلك وضعت يدي", "english": "One of them said: \"Would that my mother had never given birth to me.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1061", "section_title_en": "The States of the Fearful — Accounts of the Companions and Early Saints (Section 1061)", "section_index": 1061, "anchors": "- Root: خوف (kh-w-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of dread and apprehension; the heart's agitation before an anticipated harm.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخوف: توقع مكروه عن أمارة مظنونة، أو معلومة\" — fear is the expectation of misfortune based on a probable or certain sign; it is the opposite of security (amn) and pertains to both worldly and otherworldly matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خفته على مالي خوفا وخيفة\" — to fear for something. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تخوفه: تنقصه وأخذ من أطرافه\" — fear that consumes one gradually, as in the verse of Zuhayr where the journey \"wears away\" the camel's hump little by little, as if in dread of it.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A measure of time from sunrise to sunset, or any period whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the Day is a term for any span of time; when added to God (أيام الله), it signifies His mighty acts and visitations upon the nations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a single day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — \"days\" denotes battles and momentous events; \"أيام الله\" are His punitive calamities upon the disbelievers.\n- Root: حتي (ḥ-t-y)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A particle of end-point (ghāyah), signifying the terminal limit of an action or state, as in {ليسجننه حتى حين} and {حتى مطلع الفجر}.\n  * Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional and particle interposition (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — the particle ḥattā governs what follows as the culminating limit of the preceding narrative, marking the climax of an emotional or physical state (e.g., \"until he fell unconscious,\" \"until he died\").\n- Root: بكى (b-k-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weeping with tears; the physical manifestation of inner anguish.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Weeping is the outward expression of the heart's grief (ḥuzn); in the fearful, it is the release of the soul's dread before God's majesty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shedding tears. Metaphorical: \"بكاء الثكلي\" — the weeping of a bereaved mother, intensified by burning loss, used here to describe the saints' weeping on the Day of Arafah.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "93": [{"arabic": "وهو أحد والآصال التي ذكرها الله تعالى إذ قال ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال وإذا سجد لله عز وجل الجمادات فكيف يجوز أن يغفل العبد العاقل عن أنواع العبادات الورد السادس إذا دخل وقت العصر دخل وقت الورد السادس وهو الذي أقسم الله تعالى به فقال تعالى {والعصر} هذا أحد معنيي الآية وهو المراد بالآصال في أحد التفسيرين وهو العشي المذكور في قوله {وعشيا} وفي قوله {بالعشي والإشراق} وليس في هذا الورد صلاة إلا أربع ركعات بين الأذان والإقامة كما سبق في الظهر ثم يصلي الفرض ويشتغل بالأقسام الأربعة المذكورة", "english": "I say: And just as knowledge and remembrance are the nourishment of the heart, which can never be cut off from it, so too the measure of moderation is this; and deficiency from it may lead to the disturbance of the body — except for one who accustoms himself to wakefulness gradually, for he may train himself upon it without disturbance. And this litany (wird) is among the longest of the litanies and the most delightful for the worshippers, and it is one of the \"evenings and mornings\" (al-āṣāl) which God Most High mentioned when He said: {«ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال»} \"And to God prostrates whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and their shadows, in the mornings and the evenings\" (Q. 13:15). And when inanimate objects prostrate to God, Mighty and Majestic, how then can the intelligent servant be heedless of the varieties of worship?", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 261", "section_title_en": "The Seventh Daily Litany (Al-Wird al-Sābiʿ): The Time of Sunset — The Completion of the Day's Litanies and Self-Accounting", "section_index": 261, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, seeking it, and drinking from it; also the portion or share of water allotted for drinking at a specific time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — the core meaning is the intentional approach to water; the litany (wird) is the appointed portion of devotional recitation, likened to the daily share of water that a person must take to sustain life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the act of arriving at water. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد علي كتاب سرني مورده\" — a book arrived upon me whose arrival pleased me; likewise, the wird is the arrival of the appointed time for worship, and the soul's approach to the fountain of divine remembrance.\n- Root: عصر (ʿaṣr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes pressing, squeezing, and extracting; also the time of afternoon, so called because it is the pressing of the day's end.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ʿaṣr is the latter part of the day; God swore by it in {«والعصر»} (Q. 103:1), indicating its great merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the afternoon time. Metaphorical: the pressing of one's life — as the day is pressed at its end, so the life of man is pressed at its term.\n- Root: غرب (gharaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes disappearance, absence, and setting; the sun's setting (ghurūb) is its concealment from the eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The setting of the sun marks the boundary between the day's works and the night's; it is the terminus of the visible world's diurnal cycle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the sun's descent below the horizon. Metaphorical: the setting of the sun of life — the death of man, after which there is no rising.\n- Root: حاسب (ḥāsaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes reckoning, counting, and taking account; muḥāsabah is the mutual reckoning between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-accounting (muḥāsabat al-nafs) is the soul's reckoning with itself over its deeds before the Day of Reckoning, for \"one who reckons himself before he is reckoned\" is among the wise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the arithmetic counting of sums. Metaphorical: the soul's audit of its own works, weighing the day's deeds against the standard of the Sacred Law.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in the construction \"فسبحان الله حين تمسون\" where the adverbial clause is interposed to emphasize the temporal specification of the act of glorification.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "ونمجده تمجيد من يصرح بأن كل شيء ما سوى الله باطل ولا يتحاشى وأن كل من في السموات والأرض لن يخلقوا ذبابا ولو اجتمعوا له ولا فراشا ونشكره إذ رفع السماء لعباده سقفا مبنيا ومهد الأرض بساطا لهم وفراشا وكور الليل على النهار فجعل الليل لباسا والنهار معاشا لينتشروا في إبتغاء فضله وينتعشوا به عن ضراعة الحاجات انتعاشا ونصلي على رسوله الذي يصدر المؤمنون عن حوضه رواء بعد ورودهم عليه عطاشا وعلى آله وأصحابه الذين لم يدعوا في نصرة دينه تشمرا وانكماشا وسلم تسليما كثيرا أما بعد فإن رب الأرباب ومسبب الأسباب جعل الآخرة دار الثواب والعقاب والدنيا", "english": "It is related from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, who said: The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"No woman harms her husband in this world but that his wife from the ḥūr al-ʿīn says to her: 'May God slay you! Do not harm him — for he is only a sojourner (dakhīl) with you; soon he shall part from you and come to us.'\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Earning", "section_title_ar": "Section 311", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Earning and Livelihood — Introduction and Chapter Divisions", "section_index": 311, "anchors": "- Root: زوج (z-w-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Pairing, coupling; each of the two members of a pair — whether male/female in animals, or two matched things like sandals; \"زوجة\" is a weak (radī'ah) dialectal form.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Every one of two paired things — in animals, the male and female; in other things, any two that are paired, similar or opposite. From this: the wife is called \"zawj\" for the husband, and he \"zawj\" for her.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): \"He is her zawj and she is his zawj or zawjah; the two are zawjān.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"The two discourses paired (tazāwajā) and were coupled (izdawajā).\"\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A small creature; \"Umm risālah\": the carrion kite. The root conveys sending forth with gentleness and ease.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The origin of \"risl\" is emission with leisurely ease — a she-camel that is \"raslah\" is easy-paced. From this, the messenger (rasūl) is one sent forth; and from the sense of gentleness comes \"on your leisure\" (alā rislik).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sending a message, dispatching messengers, releasing a stallion among camels, loosing a dog or falcon upon game. Metaphorical: \"God loosed (arsala) chastisement upon them\"; \"the arrows are the messengers (rusul) of deathly fates.\"\n- Root: داب (d-'-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To persist and toil; \"al-dābbatān\" (the two toiling ones) is said of night and day in their ceaseless alternation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Da'b\" is the continuance of journeying — \"and He subjected the sun and moon to you, both persisting (dā'ibayn)\" {«وَسَخَّرَ لَكُمُ الشَّمْسَ وَالْقَمَرَ دَائِبَيْنِ»}; and \"da'b\" is the habitual custom one persists upon — \"like the custom (ka-da'bi) of the house of Pharaoh.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to strive diligently in work, to toil in travel. Metaphorical: \"This is your da'b\" — i.e., your constant concern and occupation; \"night and day toil (yad'abān) in their mutual succession.\"\n- Root: الي ('-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-alak\" and \"al-ālūk\": the chewing and working of the bit in a horse's mouth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Khalīl said: \"al-ma'lakah\" (the message/angelic mission) is the conveying of a message, because it is \"worked in the mouth\" (tu'lak fī al-fam) — from their saying of a horse: \"it works (ya'lik) the bit,\" i.e., chews it. The angels are called \"malā'ikah\" because they convey messages.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating any extension beyond it. Applied here to the wife's mourning obligation: \"she must not mourn (lā taḥidd) over any dead person more than three days — except over a husband, four months and ten days,\" where the exception clause (istithnā') delimits the general prohibition.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Earning'", "badge": "Insight"}], "98": [{"arabic": "تدميرا فقال تعالى هل تحس منهم من أحد الآية وقد حذر الله تعالى من مكره واستدراجه فقال فلا يأمن مكر الله إلا القوم الخاسرون وقال تعالى ومكروا مكرا ومكرنا مكرا وهم لا يشعرون وقال عز وجل ومكروا ومكر الله والله خير الماكرين وقال تعالى إنهم يكيدون كيدا وأكيد كيدا فمهل الكافرين أمهلهم رويدا فكما لا يجوز للعبد المهمل أن يستدل بإهمال السيد إياه وتمكينه من النعم على حب السيد بل ينبغي أن يحذر أن يكون ذلك مكرا منه وكيدا مع أن السيد لم يحذره مكر نفسه فبأن يجب ذلك في حق الله تعالى مع تحذيره استدراج", "english": "As for knowing it by way of authoritative transmission and affirmation (taṣdīq): it is that one believes in the Book of God — exalted is He — and affirms His Messenger. For God — exalted is He — has said: {«أيحسبون أن ما نمدهم به من مال وبنين * نسارع لهم في الخيرات بل لا يشعرون»} — \"Do they reckon that what We extend to them of wealth and sons — [that] We hasten for them good things? Nay, but they do not perceive\" (Q. 23:55-56). And He said — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — \"We shall draw them on by degrees from whence they do not know\" (Q. 7:182). And He said — exalted is He: {«فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أخذناهم بغتة فإذا هم مبلسون»} — \"We opened upon them the gates of everything, until when they rejoiced in what they were given, We seized them suddenly, and behold, they were in despair\" (Q. 6:44). And in the interpretation of His saying — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — it is said: \"Whenever they innovated a sin, We innovated for them a blessing, to increase their self-deception.\" And He said — exalted is He: {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} — \"We only grant them respite that they may increase in sin\" (Q. 3:178). And He said — exalted is He: {«ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} — \"And do not reckon God heedless of what the wrongdoers do; He only delays them to a Day when eyes will stare fixedly\" (Q. 14:42). And [there is] more of that kind which has come in the Book of God — exalted is He — and in the Sunnah of His Messenger. So whoever believes in it is delivered from this self-deception. For the origin of this self-deception is ignorance of God and of His attributes; for whoever knows Him does not feel secure from His stratagem (makr) and is not deceived by such corrupt fancies.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 888", "section_title_en": "Section 888 — On the Remedy for Self-Deception (Ghurūr): Recognizing the Signs of Divine Honor (Karāmah) and Humiliation (Hawān)", "section_index": 888, "anchors": "- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of an outward trace or mark; heedlessness in wakefulness; the blaze on a horse's forehead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ghurūr\" is delusion stemming from heedlessness — the soul's being overtaken while imagining security, as in {«ما غرك بربك الكريم»} (Q. 82:6). It is the state of one who is deceived by apparent favors while blind to their underlying test.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): heedlessness and exposure to predation, like a gazelle deceived by moonlight. Metaphorical (Majaz): being \"deceived by God\" (ightirār billāh) — trusting in His mercy while persisting in disobedience.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Swallowing whole; the gullet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ilhām\" — God's casting a meaning into the heart (al-raw'), distinct from demonic whispering; the channel of inner vision for the knowers (ʿārifūn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to swallow. Metaphorical: the heart's receptive \"swallowing\" of divine inspiration.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ḥudūth\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"muḥdith\" — the originator, exclusively God for substances (jawāhir). \"Iḥdāth\" — bringing about a novel state or act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: temporal newness. Metaphorical: \"muḥaddath\" — one inspired with correct discernment, as if newly spoken-to.\n- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Softness, ease, well-being; a state of comfort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Niʿmah\" — the good state one inhabits; \"naʿīm\" — the act of living pleasantly. Blessings are both a test and a sign, per {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} (Q. 16:18).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bounty and comfort. Metaphorical: \"khafat naʿāmatuhum\" — their prosperity vanished; blessings may signal either favor or gradual ruin (istidrāj).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication with \"innamā\")\n  * In {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} (Q. 3:178) and {«إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} (Q. 14:42), \"innamā\" restricts the purpose of divine respite exclusively to increase of sin and deferred reckoning — negating any reading of it as benevolent indulgence.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولا ذكر من ذكرك كما أتى على الملوك الذين كانوا من قبلك فهل تحس منهم من أحد أو تسمع لهم ركزا فكيف تبيعين يا نفس ما يبقى أبد الآباد بما لا يبقى أكثر من خمسين سنة إن بقى هذا إن كنت ملكا من ملوك الأرض سلم لك الشرق والغرب حتى أذعنت لك الرقاب وانتظمت لك الأسباب كيف ويأبى إدبارك وشقاوتك أن يسلم لك أمر محلتك بل أمر دارك فضلا عن محلتك فإن كنت يا نفس لا تتركين الدنيا رغبة في الآخرة لجهلك وعمى بصيرتك فما لك لا تتركينها ترفعا عن خسة شركائها وتنزها عن كثرة عنائها وتوقيا من سرعة فنائها أم ما لك لا تزهدين في قليلها بعد أن زهد فيك كثيرها وما لك تفرحين بد", "english": "...from creation, but rather you attain your goal through every stratagem. And by this ignorance you deserve the title of foolishness from the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, where he said: \"The shrewd one is he who holds his soul to account and works for what comes after death, and the foolish one is he who follows his soul's whims and harbors vain hopes upon God.\" {«الكيس من دان نفسه وعمل لما بعد الموت والأحمق من أتبع نفسه هواها وتمنى على الله الأماني»}", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1253", "section_title_en": "Section 1253 — The Soul's Self-Reproach and the Awakening from Negligence", "section_index": 1253, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates and is addressed as an independent moral agent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the locus of divine address and self-knowledge; its essence (dhāt) is the referent of God's \"self\" in revelation, yet here it is the reproaching subject.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage as the speaking, desiring self; metaphorical (majāz) extension to blame and counsel, as in \"self-accusation\" (lawwāmah).\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of life; the inevitable terminus of every soul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as separation (furqah) from worldly attachments and the transition to reckoning; linked to the soul's journey.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal death of the body; metaphorical \"death\" of the heart through heedlessness, contrasted here with physical death as the ultimate wake-up call.\n- Root: جهل (j-h-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Ignorance as the absence of knowledge; folly and rashness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ignorance as the soul's failure to perceive true realities, leading to misplaced desires and false hopes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal ignorance of facts; metaphorical ignorance as moral stupidity, as when the soul follows whims despite clear signs.\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Drawing near (dunūw) to God through obedience, contrasted with the soul's clinging to the near (dunyā) world.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal proximity; metaphorical use for the \"lower\" world (dunyā) as that which is nearer and inferior.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize exclusive causation, e.g., \"God's generosity is only in guiding you to the path,\" restricting divine favor to guidance, not exemption from effort.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 400, 12053]], "2": [[1, 350, 1075], [2, 1075, 2034], [3, 2034, 2911], [4, 2911, 3929], [5, 3929, 6219]], "3": [[1, 350, 952], [2, 952, 2521], [3, 2521, 3476], [4, 3476, 5296], [5, 5296, 6950]], "4": [[1, 400, 1389], [2, 1389, 2105], [3, 2105, 3446], [4, 3446, 4331], [5, 4331, 5581], [6, 5581, 6870], [7, 6870, 8484], [8, 8484, 9877], [9, 9877, 11257], [10, 11257, 12168], [11, 12168, 13001], [12, 13001, 15516], [13, 15516, 16232], [14, 16232, 17957]], "5": [[1, 400, 2189], [2, 2189, 3023], [3, 3023, 4960], [4, 4960, 5498], [5, 5498, 7909], [6, 7909, 9550], [7, 9550, 11164], [8, 11164, 12940], [9, 12940, 13788], [10, 13788, 14461], [11, 14461, 14999], [12, 14999, 16277], [13, 16277, 18087]], "6": [[1, 400, 1765], [2, 1765, 2906], [3, 2906, 3353], [4, 3353, 4114], [5, 4114, 5546], [6, 5546, 6922], [7, 6922, 7537], [8, 7537, 8997]], "7": [[1, 400, 3696], [2, 3696, 5116], [3, 5116, 6766], [4, 6766, 8037], [5, 8037, 8930], [6, 8930, 10688], [7, 10688, 11229], [8, 11229, 12433], [9, 12433, 13150], [10, 13150, 13691], [11, 13691, 14624], [12, 14624, 16337]], "8": [[1, 400, 1396], [2, 1396, 2117], [3, 2117, 3703],